WEBVTT

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:43.640
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. And we've been talking here at the radio show recently about a certain genre of movie, which is the "making of" movie. A movie about the making of another film, usually a much more famous one. A classic is Hearts of Darkness, which is a "making of" documentary about the film Apocalypse Now, which has footage that the director Francis Ford Coppola's wife Eleanor shot at the time the movie was being filmed. Plus, incredibly, these recordings that she made of her husband without him knowing it, where he's talking about his self-doubt, his despair.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》，我是艾拉·格拉斯。最近我们在节目里一直在讨论一种电影类型，那就是“电影拍摄幕后”纪录片。这是一种关于另一部电影拍摄过程的电影，通常被记录的电影会更加出名。经典之作《黑暗之心：一部电影制作的启示》就是关于电影《现代启示录》的拍摄纪录片，其中包含了导演弗朗西斯·福特·科波拉的妻子埃莉诺在电影拍摄期间拍摄的素材。此外，令人难以置信的是，她还在丈夫不知情的情况下录下了他关于自我怀疑和绝望的谈话。

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:47.980
<v Ira Glass>(SUBJECT) FRANCIS FORD
<v Ira Glass>弗朗西斯·福特

00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:53.500
<v Ira Glass>There are candid moments with the actors, Dennis Hopper, Laurence Fishburne, who's 14 years old when they were making that film.
<v Ira Glass>片中还有演员们私下的坦诚时刻，比如丹尼斯·霍珀，还有拍摄那部电影时年仅14岁的劳伦斯·菲什伯恩。

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:53.800
<v Ira Glass>[ACTOR'S INDISTINCT CHATTER]
<v Ira Glass>[演员含糊的交谈声]

00:00:53.800 --> 00:01:14.050
<v Ira Glass>Martin Sheen, who plays the main character, has a heart attack when they're there, at 36. Marlon Brando, the film's most expensive star. At some point, Coppola realizes that Brando has not read the book that the movie is based on, Heart of Darkness. So he does not get who his character is. And he's being paid $1 million a week.
<v Ira Glass>饰演主角的马丁·辛在拍摄期间突发心脏病，当时他才36岁。马龙·白兰度是片中最昂贵的明星。科波拉在某个时刻意识到，白兰度根本没读过电影原著《黑暗之心》，所以他完全不理解自己饰演的角色是谁。而他当时的片酬是每周100万美元。

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:35.410
<v Ira Glass>And all throughout, there are hints and glimpses of what's really happening in reality in the place that they're filming this movie. They're in the Philippines, where a US-supported autocrat is fighting a war against a communist insurgency. The movie's helicopters are US-made choppers flown by pilots in the Philippine military who keep getting pulled away to go back to the real war.
<v Ira Glass>整个过程中，随处可见电影拍摄地现实状况的端倪。他们当时身处菲律宾，那里一位受美国支持的独裁者正在与共产主义叛军作战。电影中的直升机是美制机型，由菲律宾军方的飞行员驾驶，他们经常被抽调走，回到真正的战场去。

00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:41.290
<v Ira Glass>(SUBJECT) FRANCIS FORD
<v Ira Glass>弗朗西斯·福特

00:01:41.290 --> 00:02:06.460
<v Ira Glass>The film Apocalypse Now is about Vietnam, but it's a big, mythic version of Vietnam, full of larger-than-life characters. The "making of" film reveals the normal-sized people building this grand cathedral, brick by brick. You see the ridiculous lengths everybody had to go through to create their pretend war and their fake, impossible Vietnam. Which brings me to today's radio show.
<v Ira Glass>电影《现代启示录》讲的是越南战争，但那是一个宏大、神话般的越南，充满了超越常人的角色。而这部拍摄纪录片揭示了那些平凡的人是如何一块砖一块砖地建造这座宏伟教堂的。你看到了每个人为了创造他们那虚构的战争和那个虚假的、不可能的越南所付出的荒谬代价。这就引出了今天的话题。

00:02:06.460 --> 00:02:40.620
<v Ira Glass>Today's show is a "making of" of a different kind. It's people creating a picture of a place and a war that's happening there. And that place is Portland, Oregon. We're going to talk about Portland as a concept, what the city has come to mean in this political moment. Back in September, you may remember, President Trump declared on Truth Social that he was going to send troops into what he called "war-ravaged Portland" to protect ICE facilities there that were, quote, "under siege from attack by Antifa." Portland came to his attention, he said, by chance.
<v Ira Glass>今天的节目是另一种形式的“幕后”。人们正在为一个正在发生的地点和战争构建某种图景。那个地点就是俄勒冈州波特兰市。我们将讨论作为一种概念的波特兰，以及这座城市在这个政治时刻所代表的意义。可能你还记得，去年九月，特朗普总统在“真实社交”平台上宣布，他要派遣军队前往他所谓的“饱受战争蹂躏的波特兰”，以保护那里“遭到反法西斯主义者（Antifa）围攻”的移民及海关执法局（ICE）设施。他说，波特兰是偶然进入他视野的。

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:46.480
<v Donald Trump>That was not on my list, Portland, but when I watched television last night, this has been going on.
<v Donald Trump>那本来不在我的名单上，波特兰，但当我昨晚看电视时，这种情况一直在发生。

00:02:46.480 --> 00:03:08.190
<v Ira Glass>Protesters had actually been outside the ICE facility since June, but Fox News, the night before, had run a story about Portland. And one thing about Fox's coverage, including that story, is that Fox's coverage includes lots of footage of protesters that is not filmed by professional TV news crews, but by the real auteurs behind the right wing's idea of what Portland is.
<v Ira Glass>示威者其实从六月份起就一直在ICE设施外抗议，但福克斯新闻前一天晚上播出了一篇关于波特兰的报道。福克斯报道的一个特点，包括那篇报道在内，就是包含了大量示威者的视频，这些视频并非由专业的电视新闻摄制组拍摄，而是由那些在右翼眼中定义波特兰的真正“作者”们拍摄的。

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:29.270
<v Ira Glass>These are right-wing citizen journalists who've been there at the protests, covering them more than anybody, especially live streamers who were there. These are people who believe in Trump, believe in ICE, who streamed from the Portland protests, saying that their lefty local government was soft on the protesters, letting Antifa run amok. Live streaming on YouTube and X.
<v Ira Glass>这些人是右翼公民记者，他们一直身处抗议现场，比任何人都报道得更频繁，特别是那些在那里进行直播的人。这些人信仰特朗普，支持ICE，他们在波特兰抗议现场直播，声称当地的左翼政府对示威者软弱无力，任由反法西斯主义者胡作非为。他们在YouTube和X上进行直播。

00:03:29.270 --> 00:03:34.510
<v Right-Wing Streamer>All these people right here are the Antifa of Portland, all in black.
<v Right-Wing Streamer>就在这里的所有人都是波特兰的反法西斯主义者，全都穿着黑衣服。

00:03:34.510 --> 00:03:36.590
<v Protester 1>You come back, I'm going to fucking smoke you, dude.
<v Protester 1>你再敢回来，老子绝对崩了你，伙计。

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:37.230
<v Right-Wing Streamer>Really?
<v Right-Wing Streamer>真的吗？

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:37.530
<v Protester 1>Yes.
<v Protester 1>是的。

00:03:37.530 --> 00:03:41.910
<v Ira Glass>Some of the citizen journalists became regular guests on Fox, talking about this.
<v Ira Glass>一些公民记者成为了福克斯新闻的常客，讨论这个问题。

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:45.910
<v Ira Glass>(SUBJECT) FOX NEWS
<v Ira Glass>福克斯新闻

00:03:45.910 --> 00:03:51.410
<v Right-Wing Streamer>It's a complete lie. There's lots of violence going on. I mean, these people are very dangerous, and they're unhinged. Just the other night--
<v Right-Wing Streamer>这完全是谎言。这里发生了很多暴力事件。我的意思是，这些人非常危险，而且精神失常。就在前天晚上——

00:03:51.410 --> 00:04:02.620
<v Ira Glass>And a week and a half after the president declared Portland to be war-ravaged and under siege, he invited these streamers to the White House for a roundtable on Antifa. It was remarkable.
<v Ira Glass>在总统宣布波特兰饱受战争蹂躏并处于被围攻状态一周半后，他邀请这些直播者到白宫参加关于反法西斯主义者的圆桌会议。这很了不起。

00:04:02.620 --> 00:04:08.580
<v Donald Trump>At least three of these courageous journalists have personally been victims of Antifa attacks
<v Donald Trump>至少有三位勇敢的记者亲身遭受了反法西斯主义者的袭击。

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:31.720
<v Ira Glass>Trump sat at the center of a U-shaped table with about a dozen of these reporters and streamers for an hour and a half. And flanking Trump at the table on his immediate left and right was the full political and law enforcement firepower of the federal government. Kristi Noem, head of the Department of Homeland Security, head of the FBI, Kash Patel, Attorney General Pam Bondi, Secretary of State Marco Rubio.
<v Ira Glass>特朗普坐在U型桌的中央，周围有大约十几名记者和直播者，会议持续了一个半小时。坐在特朗普左右两边的是联邦政府全部的政治和执法力量。国土安全部部长克里斯蒂·诺姆、联邦调查局局长、卡什·帕特尔、司法部长帕姆·邦迪、国务卿马尔科·卢比奥。

00:04:31.720 --> 00:04:39.980
<v Ira Glass>After the president's opening remarks, Kristi Noem, head of DHS, fresh off the plane from Portland herself, talked directly to the streamers.
<v Ira Glass>在总统开场致辞后，刚从波特兰飞抵的国土安全部部长克里斯蒂·诺姆直接对直播者们发表了讲话。

00:04:39.980 --> 00:05:01.890
<v Kristi Noem>I want to thank the new journalists here today for telling their stories and for being able and willing to go to the streets and to cover what's happening here in America. Many times, the legacy media has looked the other way, refused to tell the stories and how this network of Antifa is just as sophisticated as MS-13, as TDA, as ISIS, as Hezbollah, as Hamas, as all of them.
<v Kristi Noem>我要感谢今天在场的这些新晋记者，感谢你们讲述自己的故事，感谢你们愿意走到街头，报道美国正在发生的事情。很多时候，传统媒体选择视而不见，拒绝讲述这些故事，而这个反法西斯主义者网络和MS-13、TDA、ISIS、真主党、哈马斯一样复杂，和他们是一丘之貉。

00:05:01.890 --> 00:05:29.190
<v Ira Glass>When the streamers spoke, it was in apocalyptic terms about Antifa, this supposed global network of anti-government anti-fascists. Streamers talked not just about being personally assaulted by Antifa protesters, but putting their lives on the line every day. One streamer said that she genuinely believed that if the president had not declared war on Antifa, people at this table would have been killed in Portland. Here's one of the best known citizen journalists covering Portland, Nick Sortor. He's been on Fox a bunch.
<v Ira Glass>当直播者发言时，他们用世界末日般的词汇来形容反法西斯主义者这个所谓的反政府、反法西斯全球网络。直播者谈论的不仅是被示威者亲自攻击，还包括他们每天冒着生命危险进行报道。一位直播者说，她真诚地相信，如果总统没有宣布向反法西斯主义者开战，桌子上的这些人早就死在波特兰了。这是报道波特兰最知名的公民记者之一，尼克·索托尔。他多次上过福克斯新闻。

00:05:29.190 --> 00:05:47.250
<v Nick Sortor>Frankly, the cities and police departments are cooperating with Antifa, such as Portland. It's sickening. I've seen it firsthand, obviously. And President Trump, you mentioned that flag. Remember, you put out a Truth right after I--
<v Nick Sortor>坦率地说，像波特兰这样的城市和警察部门正在与反法西斯主义者合作。这令人作呕。显然，我亲眼目睹了这一点。特朗普总统，你提到了那面旗帜。记得吗，就在我之后，你发布了一条“真实”……

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:49.010
<v Donald Trump>That's right, I saw that.
<v Donald Trump>没错，我看到了。

00:05:49.010 --> 00:05:52.380
<v Nick Sortor>--took this flag from that man that was burning it in the street.
<v Nick Sortor>……从那个在街上焚烧旗帜的人手中夺过了这面旗帜。

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:53.740
<v Donald Trump>I saw. Do you know who he is?
<v Donald Trump>我看到了。你知道他是谁吗？

00:05:53.740 --> 00:05:55.140
<v Nick Sortor>Oh, yeah. I know exactly who it is.
<v Nick Sortor>噢，是的。我非常清楚他是谁。

00:05:55.140 --> 00:05:58.780
<v Donald Trump>So why don't you give it to Pam? Give it to the attorney general and--
<v Donald Trump>那你为什么不把它交给帕姆？交给司法部长，然后——

00:05:58.780 --> 00:05:58.980
<v Nick Sortor>Mm.
<v Nick Sortor>嗯。

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:01.100
<v Donald Trump>--let's start prosecutions.
<v Donald Trump>——让我们开始起诉吧。

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:07.540
<v Ira Glass>Attorney General Pam Bondi pipes in at that point to say she's already started an investigation.
<v Ira Glass>司法部长帕姆·邦迪此时插话称，她已经开始调查了。

00:06:07.540 --> 00:06:30.980
<v Ira Glass>The purpose of all this, the point the administration was making with this event, was that in Portland, these streamers and citizen journalists were proving that the threat to America from Antifa is real and that a massive, nationwide hunt was called for. And this didn't just mean sending troops into American cities like Portland. It also meant treating Antifa as a domestic terror organization.
<v Ira Glass>这一切的目的，行政当局通过这次活动想要表达的观点是：在波特兰，这些直播者和公民记者证明了反法西斯主义者对美国的威胁是真实存在的，并且需要进行一场大规模的全国性搜捕。这不仅意味着向波特兰这样的美国城市派遣军队，还意味着将反法西斯主义者视为国内恐怖组织。

00:06:30.980 --> 00:06:55.680
<v Ira Glass>The president had just named it that, and he ordered the National Joint Terrorism Task Force to create a national strategy to investigate and prosecute them with the FBI, the Justice Department, and other agencies, and to go after left-wing groups that it says are funding political violence. The speaker at this roundtable named some of them, like George Soros's Open Society Foundations. Organizations, who, of course, deny doing any such thing.
<v Ira Glass>总统刚刚将其定性为恐怖组织，并下令全国联合反恐特遣部队与联邦调查局、司法部和其他机构制定全国战略，以调查和起诉他们，并追查那些被指控资助政治暴力的左翼团体。圆桌会议的发言人点名了其中一些组织，比如乔治·索罗斯的开放社会基金会。当然，这些组织否认有任何此类行为。

00:06:55.680 --> 00:07:10.400
<v Ira Glass>Since then, prosecutions are underway. Just this week, the FBI claimed to have disrupted a bomb plot in Southern California by anti-capitalist anarchists leftists with a group called the Turtle Island Liberation Front.
<v Ira Glass>从那以后，起诉工作已经在进行中。就在本周，联邦调查局声称挫败了一起由一个名为“龟岛解放阵线”的反资本主义无政府主义左翼团体在南加州策划的炸弹阴谋。

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:23.280
<v Ira Glass>To be clear, there is no question that there are violent leftist groups in our country, just like there are violent groups on the right. But there were already laws in place and law enforcement going after them. That's been one of the FBI's jobs forever.
<v Ira Glass>需要明确的是，毫无疑问，我们的国家存在暴力的左翼团体，就像右翼也存在暴力团体一样。但法律早已存在，执法部门也一直在打击他们。这一直是联邦调查局的工作之一。

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:38.920
<v Ira Glass>This is a new kind of crackdown, at a new scale, targeted only at political violence on the left. America is a big country. We all, from the president on down, rely on images and words from other people to understand what's going on elsewhere.
<v Ira Glass>这是一场规模空前、只针对左翼政治暴力的新型镇压。美国是一个大国。从总统到平民，我们都依赖他人的影像和言论来了解其他地方正在发生什么。

00:07:38.920 --> 00:08:06.650
<v Ira Glass>And a vision of Portland, a movie about Portland as a place that's war ravaged, a hub for Antifa, ripe for military intervention, that story, promulgated by these streamers and citizen journalists, amplified on right wing TV and social media, that story is justification for all these law enforcement policies all over the country today, along with another story, of course. The assassination of Charlie Kirk.
<v Ira Glass>关于波特兰的愿景，一部关于波特兰是一个饱受战争蹂躏、反法西斯主义者中心、成熟的军事干预目标的电影，那个由这些直播者和公民记者散布、在右翼电视和社交媒体上放大的故事，成为了今天全国所有执法政策的辩护理由，当然，还有另一个故事：查理·柯克的遇刺。

00:08:06.650 --> 00:08:37.410
<v Ira Glass>We wanted to see Portland for ourselves. We wanted to see the making of this story about what Portland is. The reality, the behind the scenes, turns out not just to be very different, but a lot more interesting than the thing that they're filming and the story that they're telling. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>我们想亲眼看看波特兰。我们想看看关于波特兰的故事是如何被编造出来的。事实证明，幕后的真相不仅大相径庭，而且比他们拍摄的影片和讲述的故事要有趣得多。请留下来。

00:08:37.410 --> 00:08:42.370
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. Act One, Living the American Stream.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。第一幕：活在美式直播中。

00:08:42.370 --> 00:09:10.620
<v Ira Glass>OK, so some basic facts before we head off to Portland. The way the president has described Portland, it might sound like an entire city under siege. But I think the first thing we need to clarify in this making of is that the entire conflict between protesters and police, the entire war, is happening on exactly one city block outside an ICE facility. Pretty much every day since June, protesters have been there opposing President Trump's immigration policies, like other protests around the country.
<v Ira Glass>好的，在我们前往波特兰之前，先了解一些基本事实。按照总统对波特兰的描述，听起来整座城市都在围攻之下。但我认为在这次“幕后”调查中我们需要澄清的第一件事是，抗议者与警察之间的全部冲突，整场“战争”，其实都发生在移民及海关执法局（ICE）设施外面的那一个街区。从六月份起，几乎每天都有抗议者在那里反对特朗普总统的移民政策，就像全国各地的其他抗议活动一样。

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:31.740
<v Ira Glass>The level of violence directed towards ICE is mostly blocking and slowing down their vehicles that are coming in and out, and small scuffles where federal police fire tear gas and pepper balls. There was one day in June when things flared up and were declared a riot. Protesters rammed a stop sign through a glass door, hit an ICE agent in the head with a rock. But that day was kind of a one off.
<v Ira Glass>针对ICE的暴力程度大多是阻挡和延缓他们进出的车辆，以及联邦警察发射催泪瓦斯和胡椒球的小规模冲突。六月份有一天情况恶化，被宣布为暴乱。抗议者用停车标志撞碎了玻璃门，用石头击中了一名ICE特工的头部。但那一天算是孤立事件。

00:09:31.740 --> 00:09:54.430
<v Ira Glass>What it's been since then on any given day is anywhere from a half dozen to 200 people with music, chanting, bullhorns. During the daytime, it can have a kind of carnival performance art vibe, a street salsa class, people in inflatable frog costumes. There's a guy dressed like Bob Ross with an easel and a pallet, doing an oil painting of the ICE officers.
<v Ira Glass>自那以后，每天的情况通常是六个人到两百人不等，伴随着音乐、口号和扩音器。白天，这可能有一种嘉年华表演艺术的氛围，有街头萨尔萨舞班，穿着充气青蛙服的人。有一个人打扮成鲍勃·罗斯，带着画架和调色板，正在为ICE警官画油画。

00:09:54.430 --> 00:10:19.070
<v Ira Glass>But then at night, it can get more confrontational with ICE. Sometimes protesters have burned American flags, graffitied outside the building, shot fireworks, and it gets confrontational between the protesters and the streamers who mix it up with them. Usually, there are anywhere between just a few streamers to maybe 30 or so. It gets really noisy and disruptive, and people living near the ICE facility complain to police about the noise.
<v Ira Glass>但到了晚上，与ICE的对抗就会升级。有时抗议者会焚烧美国国旗，在建筑外乱涂乱画，燃放烟花，抗议者与那些挑衅他们的直播者之间也会产生冲突。通常现场有几名到大约30名直播者。现场变得非常吵闹和混乱，住在ICE设施附近的人们会向警察投诉噪音。

00:10:19.070 --> 00:10:45.760
<v Ira Glass>And when the streamers and citizen journalists say that the Portland police let all this happen, there's truth to that. They haven't stopped protesters from blocking sidewalks or surrounding and delaying ICE vehicles. They've allowed them to make noise. And a judge ruled that it was OK. That was the state of things when one of our producers, Zoe Chace, flew to Portland in October with another producer, Suzanne Gaber. Here's their story.
<v Ira Glass>当直播者和公民记者说波特兰警察对此置之不理时，这确实有一定的事实依据。他们没有阻止抗议者封锁人行道，也没有阻止他们包围并延误ICE车辆。他们允许抗议者制造噪音。法官也裁定这是合法的。当我们的制作人佐伊·蔡斯与另一位制作人苏珊娜·加伯在十月份飞往波特兰时，情况就是这样。这是她们的故事。

00:10:45.760 --> 00:11:00.460
<v Zoe Chace>When we got there, it had been about two weeks after the White House roundtable about Portland and Antifa. We got there after 10:00 on a Saturday night, late October. It was cold and rainy, but there are a lot of people out here wandering up and down the sidewalk.
<v Zoe Chace>当我们到达时，距离白宫关于波特兰和反法西斯主义者的圆桌会议已经过去了两周。我们在十月的一个周六晚上十点多到达。天气寒冷多雨，但人行道上来来往往有很多人。

00:11:00.460 --> 00:11:01.120
<v Zoe Chace>[YELLING AND INDISTINCT CHATTER]
<v Zoe Chace>[叫喊声和模糊的交谈声]

00:11:01.120 --> 00:11:14.880
<v Zoe Chace>And it's not grimy protesters dragging furry tails in the mud, dancing to "I Will Survive," or whatever I was picturing the campy Antifa Portland protest scene to be at this point. What we walk into is more like a Trump rally.
<v Zoe Chace>这并不是我此前想象中的那种反法西斯主义者抗议现场：污秽的抗议者拖着毛绒尾巴在泥地里跳着《我将生存》，或者其他什么奇装异服。我们走进的场景更像是一场特朗普集会。

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:17.240
<v Zoe Chace>[CROWD CHANTING, USA]
<v Zoe Chace>[人群高喊：美国！美国！]

00:11:17.240 --> 00:11:44.530
<v Zoe Chace>I want you to picture, this is one long block with an ICE facility on one side and a tall apartment building on the other. Two long sidewalks, a big street in the middle that leads to The Old Spaghetti Factory. People are swarming both sides of the street, even late at night like this. There are a bunch of city cops in yellow slickers standing around, and all these people wandering around with their phones on long selfie sticks, talking into the phones and talking to each other.
<v Zoe Chace>我想请你想象一下，这是一个长街区，一边是ICE设施，另一边是一栋高大的公寓楼。两条长长的人行道，中间是一条通往老意大利面工厂的大街。即使是深夜，街道两旁也挤满了人。一群穿着黄色雨衣的城市警察站在周围，到处都是拿着长自拍杆、对着手机说话并互相交流的人。

00:11:44.530 --> 00:12:05.070
<v Zoe Chace>Some of these people Suz and I recognize from the White House roundtable or from appearances on Fox. But mainly, I have no idea what I'm looking at. So we meet up with this one voluble streamer-- she calls herself an analyst-- Karlyn Borysenko. 128,000 subscribers on YouTube. And she's just pointing people out to us.
<v Zoe Chace>苏和我在现场认出了其中一些人在白宫圆桌会议上出现过，或者在福克斯新闻上露过面。但主要是，我完全不知道自己在看什么。所以我们遇到了这个健谈的直播者——她自称分析师——卡琳·博里森科。她在YouTube上有12.8万名订阅者。她一直在向我们指点人群。

00:12:05.070 --> 00:12:14.530
<v Karlyn Borysenko>The guy in the mask is a MAGA guy. The guy with the yellow hat right there is Antifa. The guy with the flag is Antifa.
<v Karlyn Borysenko>那个戴口罩的是MAGA支持者。那个戴黄帽子的是反法西斯主义者。那个拿旗帜的也是反法西斯主义者。

00:12:14.530 --> 00:12:41.830
<v Zoe Chace>Normally, Karlyn's streaming from home. She's kind of a commentator about the streamers. She'll have four different live videos up from four different live streamers. And it's like, camera one, no, camera two, while walking you through what or who to watch in the videos. Karlyn's a recovered lefty, now very much on the right. She considers herself an Antifa expert. She knows all these right-wing streamers and influencers, so she's a good person to follow around.
<v Zoe Chace>通常卡琳都在家里直播。她算是这些直播者的评论员。她会同时打开四个不同直播者的视频，像是在说：镜头一，不，镜头二，同时引导你观看视频里发生了什么或者谁是谁。卡琳曾经是左翼，现在坚定地站在右翼阵营。她认为自己是反法西斯主义者专家。她认识所有这些右翼直播者和网红，所以她是一个很好的向导。

00:12:41.830 --> 00:12:47.680
<v Karlyn Borysenko>Well JD Delay is there. He's got 1.6 million subscribers on YouTube. He's in the camo hat. And he's--
<v Karlyn Borysenko>JD·德莱就在那儿。他在YouTube上有160万订阅者。他戴着迷彩帽，他正在——

00:12:47.680 --> 00:12:56.840
<v Zoe Chace>I also spot Nick Sortor, 1.3 million followers on X. Cam Higby, 216,000 YouTube subscribers. They were both at the White House.
<v Zoe Chace>我也发现了尼克·索托尔，在X上有130万粉丝。卡姆·希格比，有21.6万YouTube订阅者。他们俩都在白宫开过会。

00:12:56.840 --> 00:13:06.440
<v Karlyn Borysenko>So a lot of people out here, it's kind of like they're just showing what's happening. And I think a lot of people out here are trying to get the best footage they can, regardless of what side they're on.
<v Karlyn Borysenko>所以这里有很多人，他们似乎只是在展示正在发生的事情。我认为这里很多人都在努力获取他们能拍到的最好的素材，不管他们站在哪一边。

00:13:06.440 --> 00:13:14.560
<v Zoe Chace>And what do they-- why? Do you know what I mean? Like, why? It's not as though it's not covered.
<v Zoe Chace>他们为什么这么做？你知道我什么意思吗？我是说，为什么？这又不是没有报道。

00:13:14.560 --> 00:13:38.060
<v Karlyn Borysenko>We're in a reality show right now. And you can see the reality show from that angle or that angle, or down there. I mean, I watch the live streams for hours and hours at a time on my YouTube channel when I'm at home. And you have all these different angles. You can skip around, see what's-- it's like-- you know what it's like? It's like the Big Brother house, where you can go in the kitchen or the bedroom just to spy on people. Same thing, except we're actually in it.
<v Karlyn Borysenko>我们现在就像在真人秀里。你可以从那个角度或那个角度，或者那边看到这场真人秀。我是说，我在家时会在我的YouTube频道上连续几个小时观看这些直播。你有各种不同的角度。你可以跳来跳去，看发生了什么——它就像——你知道它像什么吗？就像《老大哥》的房子，你可以进厨房或卧室去窥探别人。是一样的，只是我们真的置身其中。

00:13:38.060 --> 00:13:47.590
<v Karlyn Borysenko>And it's addictive after a while. It's like you start-- there's characters in this story. There are people that are here all the time. So you get kind of endeared to some of them.
<v Karlyn Borysenko>久而久之这会让人上瘾。这个故事里有角色，有些经常在这里出现的人。所以你会对其中一些人产生好感。

00:13:47.590 --> 00:14:15.950
<v Zoe Chace>There's a reason so many right-wing streamers are in the same place at the same time the weekend that we're here. Since the White House roundtable, more and more of these streamers have been showing up here. Then recently, the anti-ICE protesters did this thing, which was put up a poster with a bunch of right-wing influencers' faces and handles and real names on it, saying, "don't take the bait." Meaning, don't give these guys content that they'll use as propaganda.
<v Zoe Chace>这就是为什么我们来的那个周末，那么多右翼直播者聚集在同一个地方的原因。自白宫圆桌会议以来，越来越多的这类直播者出现在这里。最近，反ICE抗议者搞了个名堂，贴了一张海报，上面印着一堆右翼网红的照片、账号名和真实姓名，写着“别上钩”。意思是，不要给这些家伙提供可以作为宣传内容的素材。

00:14:15.950 --> 00:14:26.550
<v Zoe Chace>The streamers saw that and were like, OK, well, we're now all going to come at once. And we're going to make a lot of videos of you weirdos. And they're calling it Patriot Weekend.
<v Zoe Chace>直播者们看到后反应是：好吧，那我们现在要一起过去。我们要给你们这些怪人多拍点视频。他们把这称为“爱国周末”。

00:14:26.550 --> 00:14:28.790
<v Zoe Chace>[INDISTINCT CHATTER]
<v Zoe Chace>[模糊的交谈声]

00:14:28.790 --> 00:14:39.440
<v Zoe Chace>Some people are just standing around smoking and talking in the rain. And then these big circles form, out of what seems like nowhere, with people pushing and yelling like a playground fight--
<v Zoe Chace>有些人只是站在雨中抽烟聊天。然后这些大圈子似乎凭空形成，人们像操场斗殴一样推搡叫喊——

00:14:39.440 --> 00:14:42.240
<v Man 1>We ain't listening to that pedophile music, bro.
<v Man 1>我们才不听那种恋童癖的音乐，兄弟。

00:14:42.240 --> 00:15:11.920
<v Zoe Chace>--with all these other people jostling to film the fight in the middle of the circle, which is a yelling fight, to be clear, usually not a fist fight. And the people inside the circle are filming themselves being filmed and streaming that live to their followers. It's a circle of people and phones in a fight. I walk up to a group like this, try to push my way inside to see what's in the center. It looks like a dude in a bulletproof vest streaming a video of himself yelling at a guy with a Captain America shield.
<v Zoe Chace>——周围挤满了其他人，都在争先恐后地拍摄圈子中间的冲突，明确地说，那通常是叫骂式的打斗，而不是拳脚相向。圈子里的人拍着自己被拍摄的过程，并实时直播给他们的粉丝看。这是一个由人和手机组成的纠纷圈。我走进这样一群人，试图挤进去看中间发生了什么。看起来是一个穿着防弹背心的家伙正在直播自己对着一个拿着美国队长盾牌的人叫喊。

00:15:11.920 --> 00:15:16.660
<v Chad Caton>Stolen valor. We just proved it. It's disgusting. You're a liar. You're disgusting.
<v Chad Caton>窃取荣耀。我们刚刚证明了。这真恶心。你是个骗子。你真恶心。

00:15:16.660 --> 00:15:33.580
<v Zoe Chace>Apparently, thousands of people are watching this online right now. Karlyn and I elbow our way into a different crowd, but at the center nothing's happening. It seems like some fight has just ended and the streamers who were watching the fight start talking to each other about whatever that fight was about.
<v Zoe Chace>显然，现在有成千上万的人在网上观看这个直播。卡琳和我费力挤进另一个人群，但中间什么也没发生。似乎一场争吵刚结束，观看争吵的直播者们开始互相讨论刚才那件事的起因。

00:15:33.580 --> 00:15:37.260
<v Zoe Chace>It's funny, now they're just doing a talk show of what just happened.
<v Zoe Chace>很有趣，他们现在正在把刚才发生的事情当脱口秀讲。

00:15:37.260 --> 00:15:48.930
<v Karlyn Borysenko>And that happens a lot. It's one of those things, again, it's a spectacle, right? There's something going on over here, but then you get to run over there. And maybe you get to provoke something over here. And you just never know what's going to happen, you know?
<v Karlyn Borysenko>这种情况经常发生。这又是一回事，这就是场奇观，对吧？这边发生了什么，然后你就可以跑过去。也许你还能在那边挑起一点事端。你永远不知道会发生什么，你知道吗？

00:15:48.930 --> 00:15:52.330
<v Karlyn Borysenko>(SUBJECT) HIPPIE MAGA
<v Karlyn Borysenko>嬉皮士MAGA

00:15:52.330 --> 00:15:59.010
<v Zoe Chace>This is another rando chiming in. He's dressed like The Dude from The Big Lebowski, in a full robe with beads.
<v Zoe Chace>这是另一个凑热闹的家伙。他打扮得像《谋杀绿脚趾》里的“大人物”，穿着睡袍，带着珠串。

00:15:59.010 --> 00:16:01.370
<v Zoe Chace>What's hippie MAGA?
<v Zoe Chace>什么是嬉皮士MAGA？

00:16:01.370 --> 00:16:25.610
<v Zoe Chace>(SUBJECT) HIPPIE MAGA
<v Zoe Chace>嬉皮士MAGA

00:16:25.610 --> 00:16:26.570
<v Zoe Chace>Who's The Crow?
<v Zoe Chace>谁是“乌鸦”？

00:16:26.570 --> 00:16:29.410
<v Zoe Chace>(SUBJECT) HIPPIE MAGA
<v Zoe Chace>嬉皮士MAGA

00:16:29.410 --> 00:16:33.670
<v Ira Glass>Lots of handles flying around that I don't know. Karlyn looks around.
<v Ira Glass>到处都在飞着我不知道的账号名。卡琳环顾四周。

00:16:33.670 --> 00:16:41.860
<v Zoe Chace>Is there something people are waiting for now? What is everyone doing here, you know what I mean? It's 10:30, it's really cold and rainy.
<v Zoe Chace>大家现在是在等什么吗？每个人都在这里做什么，你知道我的意思吗？现在是十点半，又冷又下雨。

00:16:41.860 --> 00:16:48.780
<v Karlyn Borysenko>Yeah, well, you can see something's about to happen over there. It looks like a whole bunch of people are walking over there, so I don't know what's about to happen, but--
<v Karlyn Borysenko>是的，好吧，你可以看到那边有些事要发生了。看起来很多人都在往那边走，所以我不知道要发生什么，但是——

00:16:48.780 --> 00:16:49.280
<v Zoe Chace>Let's go see.
<v Zoe Chace>我们去看看。

00:16:49.280 --> 00:16:54.520
<v Karlyn Borysenko>Let's go see. Uh-oh. Everyone is just standing around with their phones.
<v Karlyn Borysenko>去看看。噢，糟糕。每个人都只是拿着手机站在那儿。

00:16:54.520 --> 00:16:59.600
<v Zoe Chace>Yeah, because it's like, if there's no-- if there's no fight, at some point, then there's nothing really to film, right?
<v Zoe Chace>是的，因为如果没有——如果没有打斗，在某个时刻就没什么可拍的了，对吧？

00:16:59.600 --> 00:16:59.780
<v Karlyn Borysenko>Yeah.
<v Karlyn Borysenko>没错。

00:16:59.780 --> 00:17:02.960
<v Zoe Chace>So at some point, something has to happen or there's nothing to see.
<v Zoe Chace>所以到了某个时刻，必须得发生点什么，否则就没什么可看的。

00:17:02.960 --> 00:17:05.730
<v Karlyn Borysenko>And you know what, I've kind of seen is it's--
<v Karlyn Borysenko>你知道吗，我有点看出门道了，它是——

00:17:05.730 --> 00:17:06.420
<v Pam>What you're--
<v Pam>你正在——

00:17:06.420 --> 00:17:06.960
<v Karlyn Borysenko>OK.
<v Karlyn Borysenko>好的。

00:17:06.960 --> 00:17:08.680
<v Pam>--putting out there, shame on you.
<v Pam>——发布那些东西，为你感到羞耻。

00:17:08.680 --> 00:17:09.940
<v Karlyn Borysenko>OK, bye, Pam.
<v Karlyn Borysenko>好了，再见，帕姆。

00:17:09.940 --> 00:17:10.300
<v Pam>Shame. Shame.
<v Pam>羞耻。羞耻。

00:17:10.300 --> 00:17:12.600
<v Karlyn Borysenko>Pam is a crazy person and she likes to come and yell at people.
<v Karlyn Borysenko>帕姆是个疯子，她喜欢过来对着人叫喊。

00:17:12.600 --> 00:17:13.760
<v Pam>You're not a psychologist.
<v Pam>你又不是心理学家。

00:17:13.760 --> 00:17:29.700
<v Zoe Chace>Now someone comes up and starts scolding Karlyn. An older lady, infamous on X, The MAGA Granny. She went to prison for storming the Capitol on January 6th, but she's flipped. And now she's at every anti-Trump event you can think of. The right wingers call her Pam-tifa.
<v Zoe Chace>现在有人走过来开始教训卡琳。一位老年女士，在X上很有名，外号“MAGA老奶奶”。她因为1月6日冲击国会大厦而入狱，但她后来转向了。现在她出现在每一个你能想到的反特朗普活动中。右翼人士称她为“帕姆派反法西斯主义者”。

00:17:29.700 --> 00:17:44.230
<v Karlyn Borysenko>I don't even know what you're talking about. What are you upset about, Pam?
<v Karlyn Borysenko>我甚至不知道你在说什么。帕姆，你因为什么而不满？

00:17:44.230 --> 00:18:10.230
<v Zoe Chace>The big streamers can have tens of thousands of people watching them at one time, depending on what's going on. And they make real money from that, from paid subscribers. Fans tip them in real time. One streamer out here tonight, he got into this after streaming Fortnite. This pays more, he told us, being out here.
<v Zoe Chace>大直播者在同一时间可以有数万人观看，具体取决于正在发生什么。他们通过付费订阅赚取实实在在的钱。粉丝们会实时给他们打赏。今晚在这儿的一位直播者，他是在直播《堡垒之夜》后转行做这个的。他告诉我们，在这里直播比玩游戏赚得多。

00:18:10.230 --> 00:18:43.610
<v Zoe Chace>But most streamers are more like Mike Ross, an out-of-town streamer. I followed him around for hours. He says he made money on crypto. So this is a hobby for him, not a job. Not yet, anyway. He's a very tall white guy in a big brown sweatshirt with a phone on a tripod, so he can film from really high up. He's a mental health advocate and a disabled veteran. He goes by MentallyIdaho on X. He's relatively new here, relatively few followers, 6,000. His vibe is high school football coach.
<v Zoe Chace>但大多数直播者更像迈克·罗斯，一个外地来的直播者。我跟着他走了几个小时。他说他在加密货币上赚了钱。所以这对他是爱好，不是工作。至少现在还不是。他是一个身材高大的白人，穿着一件大棕色运动衫，手机架在三脚架上，这样他可以从很高的地方拍摄。他是心理健康倡导者和残疾退伍军人。他在X上的账号是MentallyIdaho。他在这里比较新，粉丝比较少，只有6000个。他的风格像个高中橄榄球教练。

00:18:43.610 --> 00:18:54.630
<v Mike Ross>I flew back this weekend for Patriot Weekend. And just filming and live streaming and trying to get angles that other people don't get. Because I can do this and record what other people can't do.
<v Mike Ross>这个周末我飞回来参加“爱国周末”。就是为了拍摄和直播，尝试获取别人拍不到的角度。因为我可以做到，并记录下别人做不到的东西。

00:18:54.630 --> 00:18:58.270
<v Zoe Chace>Yeah, you have a big fish pole. You can get up really high.
<v Zoe Chace>是的，你有一根大鱼竿。你可以升得很高。

00:18:58.270 --> 00:18:58.410
<v Mike Ross>Yeah.
<v Mike Ross>是的。

00:18:58.410 --> 00:18:59.350
<v Zoe Chace>Plus you're tall.
<v Zoe Chace>而且你人很高。

00:18:59.350 --> 00:19:03.970
<v Mike Ross>Yeah, so I do stuff like that. What do you think of all the chaos?
<v Mike Ross>是的，所以我做这类事。你觉得这些混乱怎么样？

00:19:03.970 --> 00:19:05.290
<v Mike Ross>[CROWD CHATTER]
<v Mike Ross>[人群交谈声]

00:19:05.290 --> 00:19:07.490
<v Zoe Chace>I just got here, honestly.
<v Zoe Chace>说实话，我刚到。

00:19:07.490 --> 00:19:09.130
<v Man 2>But you don't have an answer.
<v Man 2>但你没有答案。

00:19:09.130 --> 00:19:12.170
<v Man 3>I don't have to answer, you dumb fuck.
<v Man 3>我不需要回答，你这蠢货。

00:19:12.170 --> 00:19:45.580
<v Zoe Chace>Mike said this thing about the way people talk to each other here, how mean and juvenile and graphic and vulgar it is. It's really like walking around inside social media, like we're in the comments section in real life. Remember, every person streaming here is not alone. They have this invisible mob surrounding them, sometimes animating them like a video game avatar. Mike's chat points him in various directions, saying go here, go there, collaborating and making things happen that turn into content.
<v Zoe Chace>迈克谈到了这里人们互相交谈的方式，那种刻薄、幼稚、充满图形和粗俗的语言。这真的就像走进了社交媒体内部，就像我们生活在现实版的评论区里。记住，这里直播的每一个人都不是孤身一人。他们身边有这群无形的暴民，有时像电子游戏化身一样驱使着他们。迈克的聊天室引导他去往不同的方向，叫他去这儿，去那儿，通过协作制造出变成内容的事件。

00:19:45.580 --> 00:19:53.680
<v Zoe Chace>While I'm there with Mike, one of the streamers gets arrested, and his wife is really upset. Mike tells her they're going to get help from the chat.
<v Zoe Chace>当我和迈克在一起时，其中一名直播者被捕了，他的妻子非常难过。迈克告诉她，他们会从聊天室那里得到帮助。

00:19:53.680 --> 00:19:54.360
<v Mike Ross>Say it again?
<v Mike Ross>再说一遍？

00:19:54.360 --> 00:19:57.260
<v Woman 1>They said that he might have to pay bail to get out.
<v Woman 1>他们说他可能得交保释金才能出来。

00:19:57.260 --> 00:20:05.040
<v Mike Ross>OK. People want to know how to give money. So in a minute, we'll walk down to Tommy's chat and you can talk to both chats about that, OK? You have a Venmo or CashApp?
<v Mike Ross>好的。大家想知道怎么给钱。所以一会儿，我们走去汤米的聊天室，你可以对两个聊天室谈谈这件事，好吗？你有Venmo或CashApp吗？

00:20:05.040 --> 00:20:05.380
<v Woman 1>Yeah.
<v Woman 1>有的。

00:20:05.380 --> 00:20:05.660
<v Mike Ross>OK.
<v Mike Ross>好的。

00:20:05.660 --> 00:20:06.060
<v Woman 1>Sorry.
<v Woman 1>对不起。

00:20:06.060 --> 00:20:09.420
<v Mike Ross>We'll get you set up.
<v Mike Ross>我们会帮你搞定的。

00:20:09.420 --> 00:20:11.700
<v Woman 1>My phone's about to die.
<v Woman 1>我的手机快没电了。

00:20:11.700 --> 00:20:17.600
<v Mike Ross>Hang on, hang on, hang on, hang on, hang on, hang on, hang on. Check this out. USB-C or lightning?
<v Mike Ross>等等，等等，等等，等等，等等，等等，等等。看看这个。USB-C还是Lightning接口？

00:20:17.600 --> 00:20:18.180
<v Woman 1>C.
<v Woman 1>C口。

00:20:18.180 --> 00:20:19.100
<v Mike Ross>Here you go.
<v Mike Ross>给你。

00:20:19.100 --> 00:20:21.260
<v Zoe Chace>Whoa, you're both.
<v Zoe Chace>哇，你两种都有。

00:20:21.260 --> 00:20:27.660
<v Mike Ross>There you go. Breathe. Do me a favor and take some deep breaths.
<v Mike Ross>给你。呼吸。帮我个忙，深呼吸几次。

00:20:27.660 --> 00:20:40.630
<v Zoe Chace>Mike sees himself as a helper. He got into activism around mental health issues after a hospital staff, he says, medicated him against his will. He's been diagnosed with PTSD.
<v Zoe Chace>迈克把自己视为帮助者。他说他在医院员工违背他意愿给他用药后，开始投身于心理健康问题的维权。他被诊断出患有创伤后应激障碍（PTSD）。

00:20:40.630 --> 00:21:04.670
<v Mike Ross>Severe Complex PTSD comes from a life of trauma. So ever since I was like-- I have borderline personality. Ever since I was a kid, I've been traumatized. I've been abused. I've been picked on. I've been beaten, so, my whole life. And I go become a Marine and learn to stand up for myself, stand up for other people. You stand up for the weak, for the vulnerable. So that kind of propelled me into-- use my abilities and my skills to help people.
<v Mike Ross>严重的复合型创伤后应激障碍源于一生的创伤。所以自从我像——我有边缘性人格障碍。自从我还是个孩子，我就一直受到创伤。我被虐待。我被欺负。我被打，所以我的一生都在受苦。我去当了海军陆战队队员，学会了为自己挺身而出，为他人挺身而出。你要为弱者、为受害者挺身而出。所以那种使命感驱使我——利用我的能力和技能去帮助人们。

00:21:04.670 --> 00:21:22.630
<v Zoe Chace>A lot of people I talked to out here had oddly similar stories. Tough childhood, and then some life-defining, very American trauma that seemed to explain what they were doing down here. The war in Afghanistan, the financial crisis, the COVID lockdowns, January 6th.
<v Zoe Chace>我在这里交谈的许多人都有极其相似的故事。艰苦的童年，然后是一些定义人生的、非常典型的美国式创伤，似乎解释了他们为什么会出现在这里。阿富汗战争、金融危机、新冠疫情封锁、1月6日事件。

00:21:22.630 --> 00:21:47.920
<v Zoe Chace>And each of these people seemed to find an antidote of sorts to those events via the streaming thing, recording this real-life, live-action, political video game. Being one of the good guys. Finding a community of people not just online, but in real life, but in an online way. A little audience who cares about what you care about and thinks what you're doing is important.
<v Zoe Chace>而这些人中的每一个似乎都通过直播这件事找到了这些事件的某种解药，记录这个现实生活的真人版政治电子游戏。成为好人之一。找到一个不仅仅是网上，而且是在现实生活中以一种在线方式存在的社区。有一小群观众关心你所关心的，认为你正在做的事情很重要。

00:21:47.920 --> 00:22:10.040
<v Zoe Chace>For example, like with Mike, he's not getting along with his wife right now. He talks about that openly with his chat. She doesn't want him here streaming. She thinks he could get himself into some kind of vigilante violence situation. Mike is like, that's not me. And the chat is supportive. They ask questions. They talk about their lives. Mike's reading the comments out loud.
<v Zoe Chace>举个例子，就像迈克，他和妻子的关系目前不太好。他会在聊天室里公开谈论这件事。她不希望他在这里直播。她认为他会让自己陷入某种私刑暴力的境地。迈克说，那不是我。聊天室里的人很支持。他们问问题。他们谈论自己的生活。迈克大声读着评论。

00:22:10.040 --> 00:22:22.200
<v Mike Ross>"I was spiraling in the drain. It took a big family push, but I got help. I'm still here. Still have some very rough times, but I'm still here." That's awesome, man.
<v Mike Ross>“我曾经在深渊里打转。在家庭的大力推动下，我得到了帮助。我还在这里。虽然仍有一些非常艰难的时刻，但我还在这里。”那太棒了，伙计。

00:22:22.200 --> 00:22:24.380
<v Mike Ross>(INTERVIEWER) ZOE
<v Mike Ross>采访者：佐伊

00:22:24.380 --> 00:22:48.530
<v Zoe Chace>Some streamers are very committed to documenting, like Mike. Some people come here to argue with the other side. Some are in it for the thrill of it, like a streamer named Mo told me he used to be addicted to meth, but now he's addicted to streaming. Some people are trolls. They're here to rile the protesters up into doing something that makes them look dumb or violent.
<v Zoe Chace>有些直播者非常致力于记录，比如迈克。有些人来这里是为了与另一方争论。有些是为了刺激，比如一个叫莫的直播者告诉我他曾经对冰毒上瘾，但现在他对直播上瘾了。有些人是喷子。他们来这里是为了激怒示威者，让他们看起来很蠢或很暴力。

00:22:48.530 --> 00:23:13.970
<v Zoe Chace>Something fascinating that happens, though, when you're watching those guys, is lots of times, they're trying to prove that the protesters are the cause of the violence, but in fact, when you look at the footage, often what the footage reveals is exactly the opposite. It shows clearly that some of the violence would not be happening at all if the streamers weren't there. Here's an example from the night that we were there.
<v Zoe Chace>不过，当你观看那些家伙时，有一件引人入胜的事情发生了：很多时候，他们试图证明示威者是暴力的始作俑者，但事实上，当你查看素材时，素材往往揭示的恰恰相反。它清楚地表明，如果不是直播者在场，其中一些暴力事件根本不会发生。这里有一个我们去的那晚的例子。

00:23:13.970 --> 00:23:28.420
<v Zoe Chace>Before we got there, these guys Rhein and Cam were filming themselves, tried to come into this canopy tent the protesters set up on the sidewalk as a sort of home base. A protester in a black helmet with a red anarchist sticker on it steps in front of them.
<v Zoe Chace>在我们到达之前，这两个家伙，莱恩和卡姆，正在拍摄自己，试图进入示威者在人行道上设立的作为某种大本营的雨棚。一个戴着红色的无政府主义贴纸黑头盔的示威者走在他们面前。

00:23:28.420 --> 00:23:28.960
<v Rhein>Hey, guys.
<v Rhein>嘿，伙计们。

00:23:28.960 --> 00:23:31.900
<v Protester 1>No fascists allowed in the tent. No fascists allowed.
<v Protester 1>帐篷里不允许法西斯分子进入。不允许法西斯进入。

00:23:31.900 --> 00:23:33.860
<v Rhein>Good thing I'm not a fascist.
<v Rhein>还好我不是法西斯。

00:23:33.860 --> 00:23:41.740
<v Zoe Chace>A protester in a fuzzy skeleton suit glares at them. "I'm getting medical treatment," she says to the phones looming over her.
<v Zoe Chace>一个穿着模糊骷髅装的示威者盯着他们。“我正在接受医疗治疗，”她对那些笼罩在她头上的手机说。

00:23:41.740 --> 00:23:43.860
<v Rhein>What are you getting treated for, herpes?
<v Rhein>你治的是什么，疱疹吗？

00:23:43.860 --> 00:23:44.900
<v Protester 2>Whoa, whoa, whoa.
<v Protester 2>哇，哇，哇。

00:23:44.900 --> 00:24:10.980
<v Zoe Chace>And then in a sort of, "I'll huff and I'll puff and I'll blow your house down" move, Rhein and some streamers just bust through the tent as a group, to make the point that this is a public sidewalk, which it is. They are correct. But a guy in shark pajamas is almost knocked to the ground. As everyone spills out of the tent all together, you hear the protesters cry, "assault!" And Ryan says he got maced and pours water on his face.
<v Zoe Chace>然后，以一种类似“我吹呀吹，把你的房子吹倒”的姿势，莱恩和一些直播者成群结队地冲进了帐篷，试图证明这是公共人行道，事实确实如此。他们是对的。但一个穿着鲨鱼睡衣的家伙差点被撞倒。当每个人都从帐篷里涌出来时，你听到示威者哭喊道：“袭击！”莱恩说他被喷了防狼喷雾，往脸上倒水。

00:24:10.980 --> 00:24:11.380
<v Protester 2>Assault!
<v Protester 2>袭击！

00:24:11.380 --> 00:24:11.780
<v Protester 2>[INDISTINCT YELLING]
<v Protester 2>[模糊的叫喊声]

00:24:11.780 --> 00:24:12.820
<v Counter Protester>Whoa, whoa, whoa.
<v Counter Protester>哇，哇，哇。

00:24:12.820 --> 00:24:13.740
<v Rhein>Here we go.
<v Rhein>开始了。

00:24:13.740 --> 00:24:23.280
<v Zoe Chace>Later, Rhein took a leaf blower from the protesters' supplies and moved it down the street. I asked him about what he was doing. What's the idea behind what he's even doing?
<v Zoe Chace>后来，莱恩从示威者的补给品中拿走了一个吹叶机，并把它移到了街上。我问他为什么要这么做。他到底想干什么？

00:24:23.280 --> 00:24:25.150
<v Zoe Chace>You took a leaf blower?
<v Zoe Chace>你拿了一个吹叶机？

00:24:25.150 --> 00:24:27.350
<v Rhein>Yeah, I grabbed a leaf blower.
<v Rhein>是啊，我拿了一个吹叶机。

00:24:27.350 --> 00:24:27.710
<v Zoe Chace>OK.
<v Zoe Chace>好的。

00:24:27.710 --> 00:24:29.790
<v Rhein>And I moved it 50 feet down here.
<v Rhein>然后我把它往这边挪了50英尺。

00:24:29.790 --> 00:24:31.290
<v Zoe Chace>OK, so you just moved it, you didn't take it.
<v Zoe Chace>好的，所以你只是移了它，你没有拿走它。

00:24:31.290 --> 00:24:32.470
<v Rhein>Oh, I just did it to piss them off.
<v Rhein>噢，我只是为了惹恼他们。

00:24:32.470 --> 00:24:32.890
<v Zoe Chace>Yeah.
<v Zoe Chace>是的。

00:24:32.890 --> 00:24:33.650
<v Rhein>Yes.
<v Rhein>没错。

00:24:33.650 --> 00:24:34.730
<v Zoe Chace>That's what I'm saying.
<v Zoe Chace>我就是这个意思。

00:24:34.730 --> 00:25:03.830
<v Ira Glass>Rhein's a former Trump campaign guy and a former college football player. He's big and broad. Rhein has a track record here. The Portland police have noted that he reported he'd been attacked by protesters. He was surrounded by more than 15 of them and then pepper sprayed while on the ground one time. And the police have also seen him, quote, "antagonizing the protesters until assaulted," end quote. His X bio reads, "right-wing provocateur."
<v Ira Glass>莱恩是前特朗普竞选团队成员，也是前大学橄榄球运动员。他身材高大魁梧。莱恩在这里有前科。波特兰警方指出，他曾报告自己被示威者袭击。有一次他被15个以上的人包围，然后被摁在地上喷防狼喷雾。警方也见过他，“一直挑衅示威者直到被袭击”。他在X上的个人简介写着：“右翼挑衅者”。

00:25:03.830 --> 00:25:12.970
<v Rhein>For example, some guy goes, oh, F you fascist! I'm like, oh, seriously? I'll walk up in their face and say, what do you want? And then they'll push me and I'll push them right back, and they fall to the ground.
<v Rhein>举个例子，有人说，去你的法西斯！我就说，噢，认真的吗？我会走到他们脸上说，你想干什么？然后他们会推我，我也会推回去，然后他们就会摔倒在地。

00:25:12.970 --> 00:25:14.210
<v Zoe Chace>That's provocative.
<v Zoe Chace>那是挑衅。

00:25:14.210 --> 00:25:14.310
<v Rhein>Sure.
<v Rhein>当然。

00:25:14.310 --> 00:25:16.150
<v Zoe Chace>And that leads to violence.
<v Zoe Chace>而这会导致暴力。

00:25:16.150 --> 00:25:17.630
<v Rhein>That's one night out of an entire summer, though.
<v Rhein>不过，这只是整个夏天里的一晚。

00:25:17.630 --> 00:25:24.760
<v Zoe Chace>So you don't think-- even though you call yourself a provocateur, you don't think you're really provoking them?
<v Zoe Chace>所以你不认为——即使你自称挑衅者，你也不认为你真的在挑衅他们吗？

00:25:24.760 --> 00:25:28.120
<v Rhein>With my physical actions, no. But my presence, yes.
<v Rhein>就我的肢体动作而言，不。但我的出现，是的。

00:25:28.120 --> 00:25:28.580
<v Zoe Chace>OK.
<v Zoe Chace>好的。

00:25:28.580 --> 00:25:29.360
<v Rhein>Yeah.
<v Rhein>是的。

00:25:29.360 --> 00:25:38.080
<v Zoe Chace>And is the point of that in order to make them react so that you can prove your point that they are violent?
<v Zoe Chace>那么这样做是为了让他们做出反应，好让你证明他们是暴力的观点吗？

00:25:38.080 --> 00:25:51.880
<v Rhein>It's to prove my point that they think they own the city. And my point, I do it to take away their narrative that they can do whatever they want.
<v Rhein>这是为了证明我的观点，那就是他们以为他们拥有这座城市。而我的观点是，我这样做是为了夺回他们的叙事权，即他们想做什么就做什么。

00:25:51.880 --> 00:26:26.050
<v Zoe Chace>This is precisely, exactly how many of the streamers and right-wing influencers feel. That even if Trump is in office, there's a double standard when it comes to the left and enforcement. The January 6th protesters were cracked down on and these protesters are not. So yeah, they might get in a protester's face and taunt them or film them. If the protester responds with aggression, well, that's just revealing who they are. It's fair game.
<v Zoe Chace>这就是许多直播者和右翼网红确切的感受。即使特朗普在任，在左翼和执法问题上存在双重标准。1月6日的抗议者受到了镇压，而这些抗议者却没有。所以是的，他们可能会怼到示威者脸上嘲讽或拍摄他们。如果示威者以攻击性回应，好吧，那只是揭露了他们是谁。这是公平游戏。

00:26:26.050 --> 00:26:48.170
<v Zoe Chace>I want to pivot for a moment to say something about the cops. The cops are a great focus of the streamers' ire. They think the cops are in league with the protesters. They call them Cop-tifa, say they don't arrest the protesters enough. The protesters would disagree. The night we were there, though, everyone agrees, was a big turning point. It seemed the cops were cracking down.
<v Zoe Chace>我想稍微转个话题谈谈警察。警察是直播者愤怒的焦点。他们认为警察与示威者勾结。他们称警察为“警察派反法西斯主义者”，说他们逮捕示威者不够多。示威者则会持不同意见。然而，我们去的那个晚上，大家都同意这是一个重大的转折点。似乎警察正在采取严厉措施。

00:26:48.170 --> 00:27:14.890
<v Zoe Chace>In fact, hours before Suz and I showed up here, 30 of them had descended on the protesters' tent and demanded its removal. The tent had been cleared before, but not like this. This time, there was less warning than usual and there was just so many police. In general, people said there were a lot more police all of a sudden, patrolling everything and issuing citations. And what made this happen, the streamers will tell you, a lot of it comes down to this one video, of course.
<v Zoe Chace>事实上，在苏和我出现在这里之前的几个小时，30名警察涌向了示威者的帐篷，并要求将其拆除。帐篷之前也被清理过，但不是这样。这一次，警告比平时少，而且警察太多了。总的来说，人们说突然间警察多了很多，到处巡逻并开传票。促成这件事的原因，直播者会告诉你，当然很大程度上归功于这个视频。

00:27:14.890 --> 00:27:30.460
<v Zoe Chace>From early October, a video from Rhein, actually, featuring the most known Fox ubiquitous guy down here, Nick Sortor. He got into a scuffle with protesters. He was filming them. One of them started yelling at him, and then pushed him with an umbrella.
<v Zoe Chace>来自十月初，一段实际上是莱恩拍摄的视频，主角是福克斯新闻在这里最出名的尼克·索托尔。他和示威者发生了冲突。他当时在拍他们。其中一个人开始对他大吼大叫，然后用雨伞推他。

00:27:30.460 --> 00:27:33.080
<v Protester 3>I will fuck your ass up, punk. Get out of here.
<v Protester 3>我会把你搞得天翻地覆，混蛋。滚开。

00:27:33.080 --> 00:27:35.920
<v Zoe Chace>Then a crowd formed and he got pushed into this hole.
<v Zoe Chace>然后人群聚集起来，他被推到了这个坑里。

00:27:35.920 --> 00:27:37.340
<v Zoe Chace>[INDISTINCT YELLING]
<v Zoe Chace>[模糊的叫喊声]

00:27:37.340 --> 00:27:39.180
<v Zoe Chace>[LAUGHTER]
<v Zoe Chace>[笑声]

00:27:39.180 --> 00:27:45.900
<v Zoe Chace>It's more like a huge dip in the ground covered in grass. It's called a bioswale. It's for rain runoff.
<v Zoe Chace>与其说是坑，不如说是地面上一个巨大的长满草的洼地。它被称为生物滞留池。是为了收集雨水径流的。

00:27:45.900 --> 00:27:47.760
<v Protester 4>He fell into a swale.
<v Protester 4>他掉进了一个洼地。

00:27:47.760 --> 00:27:49.860
<v Protester 4>[LAUGHTER]
<v Protester 4>[笑声]

00:27:49.860 --> 00:28:18.230
<v Zoe Chace>Two of the protesters and Nick were arrested for disorderly conduct. Nick spent the night in jail. Donald Trump texted him, "We are behind you 100%." Basically, keep your head up, like he was Tupac. Nick ended up released and not charged with anything. Immediately afterward, the Department of Justice, the Civil Rights Division, opened an investigation into, quote, "viewpoint discrimination" by the Portland police.
<v Zoe Chace>两名示威者和尼克因扰乱治安被捕。尼克在监狱里过了一夜。唐纳德·特朗普给他发短信说：“我们100%支持你。”基本上就是说，昂起头来，就像他在对待图帕克一样。尼克最终获释，没有受到任何指控。此后不久，司法部民权司对波特兰警察局所谓的“观点歧视”展开了调查。

00:28:18.230 --> 00:28:52.570
<v Zoe Chace>Nick himself has started the process to sue the police department for $10 million, alleging systemic bias against conservatives, saying the department was "riddled with Antifa." Six days after the arrest, Nick and the other influencers were at the White House doing that roundtable. Yes-anding the president's promise to send the National Guard into Portland to police this block, because the police were not. And that got a ton of coverage on right-wing media. And that's why the streamers think the cops are acting differently tonight.
<v Zoe Chace>尼克本人已经开始起诉警察局，索赔1000万美元，声称其对保守派存在系统性偏见，称该部门“被反法西斯主义者渗透”。被捕六天后，尼克和其他网红在白宫参加了那次圆桌会议。他们附和了总统派遣国民警卫队进入波特兰管辖这个街区的承诺，因为警察没在做。而这在右翼媒体上获得了大量的报道。这就是为什么直播者认为警察今晚的行为与众不同。

00:28:52.570 --> 00:28:58.910
<v Zoe Chace>Some of them, including Mike, the vet from Idaho, think the cops are scared of all this exposure.
<v Zoe Chace>他们中的一些人，包括来自爱达荷州的退伍军人迈克，认为警察是被这种曝光吓到了。

00:28:58.910 --> 00:29:10.950
<v Mike Ross>All the streamers coming down here, just the pressure that's been built from it and Fox News talking about them, they have to enforce the law and it has to be equally. So it's brought-- it definitely, definitely change things.
<v Mike Ross>所有来到这里的直播者，仅仅是因此产生的压力，还有福克斯新闻对他们的讨论，他们必须执法，而且必须是平等执法。所以它确实——它确实改变了事情。

00:29:10.950 --> 00:29:17.660
<v Zoe Chace>But why take Mike's word for it? Guess who showed up for Patriots weekend. It's Nick Sortor, himself. He's right here.
<v Zoe Chace>但为什么要听迈克的话？猜猜谁出现在了“爱国周末”。正是尼克·索托尔本人。他就在这儿。

00:29:17.660 --> 00:29:24.040
<v Zoe Chace>What do you think, Nick? Do you think that stuff like what happened to you changed what the police were doing?
<v Zoe Chace>你怎么看，尼克？你认为像发生在你身上的这类事改变了警察的行为吗？

00:29:24.040 --> 00:29:37.040
<v Nick Sortor>I mean, it sort of brought the-- it brought nationwide attention to the issue. Obviously, my intent was to bring nationwide attention to the issue, but not in that manner.
<v Nick Sortor>我的意思是，它在某种程度上引起了——它引起了全国对这个问题的关注。显然，我的目的是引起全国对这个问题的关注，但不是以那种方式。

00:29:37.040 --> 00:29:38.420
<v Zoe Chace>Not in the manner of getting arrested.
<v Zoe Chace>不是以被捕的方式。

00:29:38.420 --> 00:30:11.160
<v Nick Sortor>Not in the manner of being arrested. But I was on Laura Ingraham's show, I guess, three hours before I was arrested. The fact that I had just done that hit and it was fresh, and then I was arrested on a Thursday night, and then that whole Friday was just nonstop coverage of the arrest. Ever since then, I mean, they've pretty much been talking about it in at least one segment of each show every night. So I mean, I've done probably six or seven prime-time Fox hits since then.
<v Nick Sortor>不是以被捕的方式。但我猜在被捕前三个小时，我还在劳拉·英格拉汉姆的节目上。事实上，我刚刚做了那个连线而且很新鲜，然后在周四晚上被捕，然后整个周五都在对逮捕进行不停的报道。从那时起，我的意思是，他们每晚在每个节目的至少一个片段中都在谈论它。所以我的意思是，在那之后我可能已经做了六七次福克斯黄金时段的连线。

00:30:11.160 --> 00:30:23.860
<v Zoe Chace>Standing here on this block full of streamers, I just want to point out this is the dream, saturation coverage, with footage these guys filmed, stamped with their handles.
<v Zoe Chace>站在这里，在这满是直播者的街区，我只想指出，这就是梦想：饱和式覆盖，加上这些人拍摄的、盖着他们账号戳的素材。

00:30:23.860 --> 00:30:38.500
<v Zoe Chace>Anyway, after all the attention Nick got for his arrest, including the Department of Justice investigation into the Portland police, it seemed to the streamers that the cops were policing the protesters more strictly, like clearing the tent and everything.
<v Zoe Chace>总之，在尼克因被捕而受到所有关注后，包括对波特兰警察局的司法部调查，直播者们认为警察对示威者的执法更加严格了，比如清理帐篷什么的。

00:30:38.500 --> 00:30:59.580
<v Zoe Chace>So we asked the police, were the streamers right? Were they policing differently after Nick Sortor? And they responded, they were doing more enforcement, but for a different reason. Quote, "If there was any change, it was in the crowd's behavior. We began to see an influx of streamers, the people who want to monetize the situation."
<v Zoe Chace>所以我们问警察，直播者说对了吗？在尼克·索托尔事件之后，他们的执法是否不同？他们回应说，他们确实在加强执法，但原因是不同的。引述：“如果有什么变化，那就是人群行为的变化。我们开始看到大量直播者的涌入，那些想要将局势货币化的人。”

00:30:59.580 --> 00:31:28.830
<v Zoe Chace>Still quoting the police here, they went on, "The increase in the number of live streamers and personalities in the area contributed to an environment where tension and confrontation escalated. Many of these individuals were creating content for audiences that reward intensity and conflict, which can sometimes amplify volatile situations." In other words, the live streamers made the protesters act up, and the police cracked down on both of them.
<v Zoe Chace>继续引用警察的话：“在该地区直播者和公众人物数量的增加，导致了一种紧张局势和对抗升级的环境。这些人中的许多人正在为奖励紧张和冲突的受众创造内容，这有时会放大不稳定的局势。”换句话说，直播者让示威者闹事，警察对双方都采取了严厉措施。

00:31:28.830 --> 00:31:42.910
<v Zoe Chace>That is definitely not how the streamers interpreted things. On this night, certainly, they just thought they were winning.
<v Zoe Chace>这绝对不是直播者对事情的解读方式。至少在这一晚，他们只是认为自己赢了。

00:31:42.910 --> 00:32:08.130
<v Zoe Chace>It's after midnight now. The streamers seem to be on a high. They've seen the cops clear the protesters' tent. The cops seem to be listening to them. They're filled with this "we're winning" adrenaline. They want to do something. So they start this march down the street towards this apartment. Something like 30-odd people marching, with their phones aloft, yelling someone's name. Chandler, Chandler.
<v Zoe Chace>现在是午夜过后。直播者似乎处于兴奋状态。他们看到警察清理了示威者的帐篷。警察似乎在听他们的话。他们充满了这种“我们赢了”的肾上腺素。他们想做点什么。所以他们开始沿着街道向那个公寓走去。大约30多个人走着，高举着手机，大喊着某人的名字：钱德勒，钱德勒。

00:32:08.130 --> 00:32:10.830
<v Zoe Chace>[CROWD YELLING CHANDLER]
<v Zoe Chace>[人群高喊钱德勒]

00:32:10.830 --> 00:32:21.800
<v Zoe Chace>They opened the door to this guy's apartment. It just opens, which is shocking to see and feels a little scary. Like, is someone going to get dragged out and beat up in some soccer hooligany way?
<v Zoe Chace>他们打开了这个家伙公寓的门。门就这么开了，这看起来很震惊，让人觉得有点可怕。就像，会不会有人被拖出来像足球流氓那样被暴打一顿？

00:32:21.800 --> 00:32:22.720
<v Counter-Protester 2>Chandler!
<v Counter-Protester 2>钱德勒！

00:32:22.720 --> 00:32:28.460
<v Counter-Protester 3>We want to have a discussion. Is anybody home? Chandler!
<v Counter-Protester 3>我们想进行讨论。有人在家吗？钱德勒！

00:32:28.460 --> 00:32:31.400
<v Counter-Protester 3>[INDISTINCT YELLING]
<v Counter-Protester 3>[模糊的叫喊声]

00:32:31.400 --> 00:32:43.340
<v Zoe Chace>I find myself next to Chad Caton, the guy in a bulletproof vest who was yelling about stolen valor before. We're just a few feet back from the apartment door. He's streaming as we're talking, which is always how it is.
<v Zoe Chace>我发现自己站在查德·卡顿旁边，就是之前那个大喊“窃取荣耀”的穿着防弹背心的家伙。我们距离公寓门只有几英尺。我们在谈话时他一直在直播，这总是他们的常态。

00:32:43.340 --> 00:32:44.920
<v Zoe Chace>What's going on here?
<v Zoe Chace>这里发生了什么？

00:32:44.920 --> 00:33:07.640
<v Chad Caton>So Chandler has been operating the Antifa out of here. They've been running this neighborhood, and these patriots have had enough. They're taking back their streets. And this has been Chandler's-- this is where they have attacked patriots and ran in here. This is a safe house. And this is where Antifa hangs out here in Rose City.
<v Chad Caton>所以钱德勒一直在这里操纵反法西斯主义者。他们一直在管理这个街区，而这些爱国者受够了。他们正在夺回他们的街道。这就是钱德勒的——这就是他们攻击爱国者并躲进去的地方。这是一个避难所。这就是反法西斯主义者在玫瑰城潜伏的地方。

00:33:07.640 --> 00:33:27.290
<v Zoe Chace>A lot of this stuff turns out not to be actual fact, but more like Chad's interpretation of things, which others would strongly disagree with. But he is speaking the way most streamers and influencers would about Chandler's apartment-- that is, Antifa headquarters. Another streamer, Nick Shirley, runs up to Chad.
<v Zoe Chace>这些东西很多后来证明并非事实，更像是查德对事情的解读，其他人会强烈反对。但他讲话的方式和大多数直播者及网红谈论钱德勒公寓的方式一样——即反法西斯主义者总部。另一个直播者，尼克·雪莉，跑到查德身边。

00:33:27.290 --> 00:33:28.690
<v Nick Shirley>What's going on? What have I missed?
<v Nick Shirley>发生什么了？我错过了什么？

00:33:28.690 --> 00:33:34.050
<v Zoe Chace>Chad catches him and his viewers up with the not exactly true things that he told me.
<v Zoe Chace>查德向他和他的观众更新了那些他告诉我的并不完全属实的事情。

00:33:34.050 --> 00:33:46.690
<v Chad Caton>They're just letting Chandler know that the bullying is done and they're taking back their streets. Yeah, this is Rose City headquarters. It's a storefront because he's a trust fund baby, and this is where they have their furry parties and all that.
<v Chad Caton>他们只是让钱德勒知道欺凌结束了，他们正在夺回街道。是的，这是玫瑰城总部。这是一家店面，因为他是个信托基金宝贝，这就是他们举行毛绒派对等等的地方。

00:33:46.690 --> 00:33:47.210
<v Nick Shirley>Really?
<v Nick Shirley>真的吗？

00:33:47.210 --> 00:33:48.450
<v Chad Caton>Mm-hmm.
<v Chad Caton>嗯哼。

00:33:48.450 --> 00:34:01.570
<v Zoe Chace>I'll just clarify, this is not a storefront. It's not the headquarters of Rose City Antifa. Chandler isn't a trust fund baby, and there don't seem to be any furry parties here, actually. But anyway--
<v Zoe Chace>我澄清一下，这不是店面。这不是玫瑰城反法西斯主义者的总部。钱德勒不是信托基金宝贝，其实这里似乎也没有什么毛绒派对。但不管怎样——

00:34:01.570 --> 00:34:05.890
<v Nick Shirley>So this is the spot where Antifa, what, they just-- their home base down here, basically?
<v Nick Shirley>所以这是反法西斯主义者——他们的大本营，基本上？

00:34:05.890 --> 00:34:11.380
<v Chad Caton>Yeah, this is where they hide. This is the safe house, known as a safe house.
<v Chad Caton>是的，这是他们躲藏的地方。这是避难所，被称为避难所。

00:34:11.380 --> 00:34:14.180
<v Woman 3>Are you a fascist? Are you a fascist? No.
<v Woman 3>你是法西斯吗？你是法西斯吗？不。

00:34:14.180 --> 00:34:27.220
<v Zoe Chace>At this point, Nick takes over from Chad, narrating for his live stream. He knows Chandler. A lot of the people watching his stream know Chandler. Any regulars who tune into the Portland scene down here know who he is.
<v Zoe Chace>此时，尼克接替了查德，为他的直播进行解说。他认识钱德勒。观看他直播的很多人都知道钱德勒。任何收听波特兰现场报道的常客都知道他是谁。

00:34:27.220 --> 00:34:40.120
<v Nick Shirley>He's honestly, seems like a very-- he's a very normal person when you talk to him and have conversations. But he has been the most well-known person for being the head of Antifa here in this part of Portland, Oregon.
<v Nick Shirley>他老实说，看起来是一个非常——当你和他交谈时，他是一个非常正常的人。但他一直是波特兰这一部分最出名的反法西斯主义者头目。

00:34:40.120 --> 00:34:42.780
<v Zoe Chace>So what are people doing outside his house right now?
<v Zoe Chace>所以人们现在在他家外面做什么？

00:34:42.780 --> 00:34:52.380
<v Nick Shirley>Yeah, well, Antifa has kind of been defeated today, as they took down his encampment. So I think people want to see what Chandler had to say and if he was going to do anything about it.
<v Nick Shirley>是的，好吧，今天反法西斯主义者已经被击败了，因为他们拆除了他的营地。所以我认为人们想看看钱德勒有什么话说，以及他是否打算对此做点什么。

00:34:52.380 --> 00:35:15.110
<v Zoe Chace>Chandler pokes his head out the door. He looks like a sleepy, tousled, blond, college kid who just rolled out of bed. He seems completely unfazed by the mass of shouting people and phones just outside his house. A few of his friends-- the protester in the fuzzy skeleton suit, another in a keffiyeh and goggles, a couple in all black-- are hovering around him.
<v Zoe Chace>钱德勒从门缝里探出头。他看起来像个睡眼惺忪、头发蓬乱、金发的大学生，刚从床上爬起来。面对屋外的叫喊人群和手机，他似乎完全没有受到干扰。他的几个朋友——那个穿着模糊骷髅装的示威者，另一个戴着头巾和护目镜的，几个全黑装扮的——都在他周围徘徊。

00:35:15.110 --> 00:35:22.990
<v Zoe Chace>Chandler stands in the middle of the open door and starts addressing the group of chanting, angry, excited, victorious people.
<v Zoe Chace>钱德勒站在敞开的门中央，开始对这群高喊着、愤怒的、兴奋的、胜利的人群发表讲话。

00:35:22.990 --> 00:35:30.890
<v Chandler Patey>It is entirely to the advantage of the powerful to have us, the working class, blame the powerless. Does that make sense?
<v Chandler Patey>让作为工人阶级的我们去责怪无权无势的人，这完全符合强权者的利益。这有道理吗？

00:35:30.890 --> 00:35:31.190
<v Man 4>No.
<v Man 4>没有。

00:35:31.190 --> 00:35:38.230
<v Zoe Chace>It is also very surprising to me to hear this Orson Welles-type voice come out of this young person's mouth.
<v Zoe Chace>听到这个年轻人嘴里发出奥逊·威尔斯式的声音，我也感到非常惊讶。

00:35:38.230 --> 00:35:52.650
<v Chandler Patey>It is entirely to the advantage of the ruling class to convince you to blame the powerless, to blame the immigrants, to blame the left, to blame the right, to blame the rural farmers, to blame the liberals in the city, to blame the conservatives, right?
<v Chandler Patey>让你们去责怪无权无势的人，去责怪移民，去责怪左派，去责怪右派，去责怪农村农民，去责怪城市里的自由派，去责怪保守派，这完全符合统治阶级的利益，对吧？

00:35:52.650 --> 00:36:21.240
<v Zoe Chace>He's ringed by streamers and phones, some of whom are just filming without comment, but a lot of whom are yelling at each other and teasing him. Because of the way Chandler talks, the whole thing feels just of another era, as though he's on a literal soapbox and people in bowler hats are on a cobblestone street, booing and throwing tomatoes at him. But it's just phones bobbing everywhere, and people narrating and arguing while he's talking.
<v Zoe Chace>他被直播者和手机包围着，其中一些人只是在拍摄而不加评论，但许多人却在互相叫喊并嘲讽他。因为钱德勒讲话的方式，整件事感觉像是另一个时代的产物，仿佛他站在真正的肥皂箱上，而戴着圆顶礼帽的人们在鹅卵石街道上对他嘘声并扔西红柿。但现场到处都是晃动的手机，人们在解说和争论，而他正在说话。

00:36:21.240 --> 00:36:52.520
<v Chandler Patey>So when your SNAP benefits run out soon, when you go hungry, remember, it is the powerful. It is not the poor. It is the Democrats. It is the Republicans. It is the ruling class. It is the rich. It is the people who determine what our economy does. Does that make sense? They own our industry. They own our fucking housing. They own the hours of your fucking life.
<v Chandler Patey>所以当你的食品券补助快用完时，当你挨饿时，记住，是掌权者造成的。不是穷人。是民主党人。是共和党人。是统治阶级。是富人。是那些决定我们经济走向的人。这有道理吗？他们拥有我们的工业。他们拥有我们的操蛋住房。他们拥有你操蛋生命里的分分秒秒。

00:36:52.520 --> 00:36:53.240
<v Nick Shirley>Thank you, Chandler.
<v Nick Shirley>谢谢你，钱德勒。

00:36:53.240 --> 00:37:07.810
<v Chandler Patey>There is no left and right. There is only rich and poor. There is only powerful and powerless. You are among the powerless. We are among the powerless.
<v Chandler Patey>没有左派和右派。只有富人和穷人。只有有权有势和无权无势。你属于无权无势的那一边。我们也属于无权无势的那一边。

00:37:07.810 --> 00:37:14.690
<v Zoe Chace>People are starting to get restless, unsure what to do next since this Chandler catharsis seems to be getting old.
<v Zoe Chace>人们开始变得不安，不确定接下来要做什么，因为这种钱德勒式的宣泄似乎已经过时了。

00:37:14.690 --> 00:37:21.010
<v Man 5>Chandler is still harboring terrorists after trying to gobble our knobs all night last night.
<v Man 5>钱德勒还在窝藏恐怖分子，昨晚整晚都在试图玩弄我们。

00:37:21.010 --> 00:37:26.810
<v Zoe Chace>Eventually, people do start wandering away from Chandler's house. Karlyn is giddy.
<v Zoe Chace>最终，人们确实开始离开钱德勒的房子。卡琳欣喜若狂。

00:37:26.810 --> 00:37:28.010
<v Zoe Chace>Is it fun, though?
<v Zoe Chace>好玩吗？

00:37:28.010 --> 00:37:35.490
<v Karlyn Borysenko>Yeah, it's a ton of fun. Aren't you having fun? Wasn't that fun? [LAUGHS]
<v Karlyn Borysenko>是的，太好玩了。你不觉得好玩吗？刚才不好玩吗？[笑]

