<v Woman 4>[LAUGHTER]
<v Woman 4>[笑声]

00:38:15.620 --> 00:38:23.460
<v Zoe Chace>It's like 30 people. Then some of them bring their phones close and bid fond farewells to their viewers online.
<v Zoe Chace>大约有30个人。然后他们中的一些人把手机凑近，向线上的观众深情告别。

00:38:23.460 --> 00:38:45.860
<v Chad Caton>I'll see you guys tomorrow. I love you guys. We won tonight, baby. We won. We're doing it right now. So I'm going to help. I love you guys. I'll make a post when I get home.
<v Chad Caton>明天见。我爱你们。我们今晚赢了，宝贝。我们赢了。我们现在正在做。所以我去帮忙。我爱你们。我到家后会发帖的。

00:38:45.860 --> 00:39:18.510
<v Zoe Chace>All the world's a stage, and all the men and women merely players. You can't help feeling like this is so performative and absurd, that even the menacing moments are just part of a drama they invented to keep whoever's watching on social media entertained. It's cartoonish. It's over the top. And the search for red Antifa on this block can mean a random ICE protester in a frog costume. It could mean that person who hit a right-wing influencer with a flagpole and then ran away.
<v Zoe Chace>世界就是一个舞台，所有的男人和女人不过是演员。你忍不住觉得这太表演性且荒谬了，即使是那些威胁性的时刻，也只是他们编造的戏剧的一部分，目的是为了让社交媒体上的观众感到娱乐。这很卡通化。这太夸张了。在这个街区寻找红色的反法西斯主义者可能意味着一个穿着青蛙服的随机ICE抗议者。它可能意味着那个用旗杆打了右翼网红然后跑掉的人。

00:39:18.510 --> 00:39:49.110
<v Zoe Chace>But here's what else the word Antifa now means in America, partly because of the story the streamers are telling about Portland. There's a great smooshing that's happening now. Protest means Antifa. Antifa means violence. And Antifa is rampant, organized, networked, well-funded, and belongs in the sizzle reel with the much more famous foreign terrorist groups that Americans all know. Remember DHS head Kristi Noem at the White House roundtable with the streamers.
<v Zoe Chace>但这就是“反法西斯主义者”这个词现在在美国的含义，部分是因为直播者讲述的关于波特兰的故事。现在发生了一种巨大的混淆。抗议意味着反法西斯主义者。反法西斯主义者意味着暴力。而反法西斯主义者猖獗、有组织、有网络、资金充足，属于那种美国人都知道的更著名的外国恐怖组织热场片。记得白宫圆桌会议上国土安全部部长克里斯蒂·诺姆对直播者说的话。

00:39:49.110 --> 00:40:00.640
<v Kristi Noem>This network of Antifa is just as sophisticated as MS-13, as TDA, as ISIS, as Hezbollah, as Hamas, as all of them.
<v Kristi Noem>这个反法西斯主义者网络和MS-13、TDA、ISIS、真主党、哈马斯一样复杂，和他们是一丘之貉。

00:40:00.640 --> 00:40:18.440
<v Zoe Chace>So let's go inside the Antifa safe house, see for ourselves, and talk to the head of Antifa, so-called. After the break, the frog guy gets us in.
<v Zoe Chace>所以，让我们进入反法西斯主义者的避难所，亲自看看，并与所谓的反法西斯主义者头目谈谈。中场休息后，那个青蛙男带我们进去。

00:40:18.440 --> 00:40:31.680
<v Ira Glass>Zoe Chace. Coming up, Chandler's apartment. Could you be any more excited? That's in a minute, Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>佐伊·蔡斯。接下来是钱德勒的公寓。你还能更兴奋吗？一分钟后，芝加哥公共广播电台，节目继续。

00:40:31.680 --> 00:40:48.340
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today's show, The Making Of. We're taking a look behind the scenes at the people who are creating a story about Portland that's fueling a national crackdown by the FBI, the Justice Department, Treasury, the IRS, DHS. We've arrived at Act Two of our program.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天的节目是《幕后》。我们正在深入幕后，看看那些正在炮制关于波特兰故事的人，这些故事正在助长FBI、司法部、财政部、国税局和国土安全部的一场全国性镇压行动。我们已经来到了节目的第二幕。

00:40:48.340 --> 00:41:02.810
<v Ira Glass>Act two, Inside the Safe House. OK, so where we left off, our producers Zoe Chace and Suzanne Gaber were trying to actually meet the so-called leader of Antifa. We pick up our story a couple nights later, outside his door.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，进入避难所。好吧，回到上文，我们的制作人佐伊·蔡斯和苏珊娜·加伯正试图真正见到所谓的反法西斯主义者头目。我们将在几天后的深夜，在他门外继续我们的故事。

00:41:02.810 --> 00:41:04.970
<v Suzanne Gaber>We've arrived.
<v Suzanne Gaber>我们到了。

00:41:04.970 --> 00:41:05.930
<v Zoe Chace>Oh we're here.
<v Zoe Chace>噢，我们到了。

00:41:05.930 --> 00:41:07.650
<v Suzanne Gaber>Yeah.
<v Suzanne Gaber>是的。

00:41:07.650 --> 00:41:10.050
<v Zoe Chace>The Antifa safe house.
<v Zoe Chace>反法西斯主义者的避难所。

00:41:10.050 --> 00:41:11.110
<v Suzanne Gaber>Are you ready to enter?
<v Suzanne Gaber>准备好进去了吗？

00:41:11.110 --> 00:41:12.770
<v Suzanne Gaber>[KNOCKING]
<v Suzanne Gaber>[敲门声]

00:41:12.770 --> 00:41:17.950
<v Zoe Chace>We're welcomed in, and the first thing we see is this sign that's posted right in front of the door.
<v Zoe Chace>我们被欢迎进去，我们看到的第一件事就是贴在门正前方的那张告示。

00:41:17.950 --> 00:41:21.930
<v Zoe Chace>"Three cameras and the Second Amendment."
<v Zoe Chace>“三台摄像头和宪法第二修正案。”

00:41:21.930 --> 00:41:23.130
<v Chandler Patey>That's a lie.
<v Chandler Patey>那是谎言。

00:41:23.130 --> 00:41:25.470
<v Zoe Chace>OK, so what does it mean?
<v Zoe Chace>好的，那是什么意思？

00:41:25.470 --> 00:41:34.470
<v Chandler Patey>It's supposed to imply that there are three cameras, and also that I have a gun, but I have neither of those things. There are no cameras in here and I don't have a gun.
<v Chandler Patey>它本意是暗示有三台摄像头，也意味着我有一把枪，但我两者都没有。这里没有摄像头，我也没有枪。

00:41:34.470 --> 00:41:35.930
<v Zoe Chace>So why did you put it up?
<v Zoe Chace>那你为什么贴出来？

00:41:35.930 --> 00:41:42.050
<v Chandler Patey>To dissuade people from walking in and, you know--
<v Chandler Patey>为了劝阻人们走进来，你知道的——

00:41:42.050 --> 00:41:42.410
<v Zoe Chace>Yeah.
<v Zoe Chace>是的。

00:41:42.410 --> 00:41:44.410
<v Chandler Patey>--doing weird, dumb stuff.
<v Chandler Patey>——做些奇怪、愚蠢的事情。

00:41:44.410 --> 00:42:23.430
<v Zoe Chace>Just a prop. No real guns, like so much else around here. So Chandler's apartment, it both looks like a safe house for a socialist and a dude living alone in his 20s. Two chairs, one desk, kitchenette. He offered us tea in measuring cups. Two bookshelves. Science fiction. Dune, Hitchhiker's Guide, Fahrenheit 451, Orwell, Vonnegut. But also, the complete works of Rosa Luxemburg, The Rise and Fall of Swedish Social Democracy. Psychology books. Chandler said he'd been planning to do cognitive behavioral science before he dropped out of college.
<v Zoe Chace>只是个道具。没有真枪，就像这里的其他很多东西一样。所以钱德勒的公寓看起来既像是一个社会主义者的避难所，也是一个20多岁独居男子的住处。两把椅子，一张桌子，小厨房。他用量杯请我们喝茶。两个书架。科幻小说。《沙丘》、《银河系漫游指南》、《华氏451》、《奥威尔》、《冯内古特》。但也全套收藏了罗莎·卢森堡的作品、《瑞典社会民主主义的兴衰》。心理学书籍。钱德勒说他在退学前曾计划攻读认知行为科学。

00:42:23.430 --> 00:42:47.350
<v Chandler Patey>My name is Chandler Patey. I'm a carpenter, and I guess I have an apartment that is right next to ICE, and I'm an activist and protester. And I can't call myself an organizer for this protest, but I do usually organize outside of this event, because there's no organization on this one. [CHUCKLES]
<v Chandler Patey>我叫钱德勒·佩蒂。我是一名木匠，我想我住的公寓就在ICE旁边，我是一名活动家和抗议者。我不能称自己为这次抗议的组织者，但我确实通常会在这次活动之外进行组织，因为这次活动根本没有组织。[笑声]

00:42:47.350 --> 00:42:51.350
<v Zoe Chace>So are you the leader of Antifa?
<v Zoe Chace>那你就是反法西斯主义者的头目吗？

00:42:51.350 --> 00:43:25.520
<v Chandler Patey>Unfortunately not. Or rather, fortunately not, because first of all, obviously, I mean, you know that doesn't make any sense. Antifa is not an organization. There are organizations that identify themselves as anti-fascist, right? But there are organizations that identify themselves as socialist or conservative, and they aren't the entirety of that thing. So it just doesn't make any sense to say that you are the leader of conservatism, or that you are the leader of socialism, because it's an ideology. You can't be the leader of an ideology. That doesn't make sense.
<v Chandler Patey>不幸的是不是。或者说，幸运的是不是，因为首先，显然，嗯，你知道那根本说不通。反法西斯主义者不是一个组织。确实有一些组织自认为是反法西斯主义的，对吧？但也有组织自认为是社会主义者或保守派的，他们并不代表那个事物的全部。所以说你是保守主义的头目，或者你是社会主义的头目，这根本说不通，因为这是一种意识形态。你不可能成为一种意识形态的头目。这没意义。

00:43:25.520 --> 00:43:56.770
<v Zoe Chace>I think it's important to be as clear as I can about this question, what is Antifa? There are organizations with the word Antifa in their name. Steel City Antifascist League, Atlanta Antifascists. One is famously from Portland, Rose City Antifa. It has a website. It has a flag. But the idea that all these different Antifa groups or anarchist groups are connected with a funding stream and a leadership, there's no public evidence that's true.
<v Zoe Chace>我认为在关于“什么是反法西斯主义者”这个问题上，我需要尽可能讲清楚。确实有一些组织在名字里带有“反法西斯主义者”这个词。钢铁城反法西斯联盟、亚特兰大反法西斯组织。一个很出名的来自波特兰，玫瑰城反法西斯组织。它有网站。它有旗帜。但认为所有这些不同的反法西斯主义团体或无政府主义团体都与资金流和领导层相连，没有任何公开证据表明这是真的。

00:43:56.770 --> 00:44:19.610
<v Zoe Chace>That was the conclusion of the FBI during the first Trump administration. Trump's former FBI director famously said there are violent, anarchist extremists who identify as Antifa in this country. But he said it was an ideology, not an organization that the FBI could target. Self-radicalized lone actors, he called them.
<v Zoe Chace>这也是特朗普第一任期内联邦调查局的结论。特朗普的前联邦调查局局长曾出名地表示，这个国家存在自认为是反法西斯主义者的暴力无政府主义极端分子。但他表示这是一种意识形态，而不是联邦调查局可以针对的组织。他称他们为“自我激进化的孤狼”。

00:44:19.610 --> 00:44:34.450
<v Zoe Chace>The current Trump administration, the second one, has obviously gone in a very different direction. So in this environment, you probably wouldn't want to be publicly identified as Antifa or the leader of Antifa, right now.
<v Zoe Chace>当前的特朗普政府，也就是第二任期，显然走向了一个非常不同的方向。所以在这个环境下，你现在可能不会想被公开确认为反法西斯主义者或反法西斯主义者的头目，对吧？

00:44:34.450 --> 00:44:37.810
<v Zoe Chace>So why are people calling you the leader of Antifa?
<v Zoe Chace>那你为什么会被人们称为反法西斯主义者的头目？

00:44:37.810 --> 00:44:59.740
<v Chandler Patey>Because there's this idiot named Velly Ray who's a streamer. He's very dumb. And I tried really hard to be nice to him for a long time, but he is just-- he's just so malicious and stupid. He just makes stuff up to be sensational. And he'll just try and overdramatize the things that happened, and sometimes that would include vilifying us.
<v Chandler Patey>因为有个叫维利·雷的白痴，是个直播者。他很蠢。我试过很长一段时间努力对他好，但他只是——他只是太恶毒和愚蠢了。他只是为了耸人听闻而编造事实。他会试图过度渲染发生的事情，有时那会包括诋毁我们。

00:44:59.740 --> 00:45:08.820
<v Zoe Chace>I asked Velly about this. And he said he's been filming Chandler for six and a half months, and he stands by his claim about him 100%.
<v Zoe Chace>我问了维利关于这件事。他说他已经跟踪拍摄钱德勒六个半月了，他100%坚持关于他的说法。

00:45:08.820 --> 00:45:20.980
<v Zoe Chace>I'd say Velly Ray is like Chandler's Moriarty, his Lex Luthor, his Joker. Except Chandler has so many nemeses, it's sort of hard to pick. Anyway, here's Velly Ray's stream from a few weeks ago.
<v Zoe Chace>我会说维利·雷就像钱德勒的莫里亚蒂，他的莱克斯·卢瑟，他的小丑。除了钱德勒有太多的宿敌，很难选一个。总之，这是维利·雷几周前的直播。

00:45:20.980 --> 00:45:25.150
<v Velly Ray>I'm following Chandler.
<v Velly Ray>我正在跟踪钱德勒。

00:45:25.150 --> 00:45:27.180
<v Velly Ray>[INDISTINCT CONVERSATION]
<v Velly Ray>[模糊的谈话声]

00:45:27.180 --> 00:45:33.140
<v Velly Ray>Chandler, I love you! Chandler! Chandler, don't go! Chandler!
<v Velly Ray>钱德勒，我爱你！钱德勒！钱德勒，别走！钱德勒！

00:45:33.140 --> 00:45:39.140
<v Zoe Chace>Someone in Velly Ray's chat posted, "You're like Brando in Streetcar Named Desire right now."
<v Zoe Chace>维利·雷聊天室里有人发帖说：“你现在就像《欲望号街车》里的白兰度。”

00:45:39.140 --> 00:45:43.300
<v Velly Ray>Chandler! Chandler, you just walked in a full circle.
<v Velly Ray>钱德勒！钱德勒，你刚刚兜了个大圈子。

00:45:43.300 --> 00:45:45.180
<v Chandler Patey>Because I was trying to walk away from you.
<v Chandler Patey>因为我正试图避开你。

00:45:45.180 --> 00:45:46.820
<v Velly Ray>You just walked in a full circle.
<v Velly Ray>你刚刚兜了个大圈子。

00:45:46.820 --> 00:46:02.910
<v Zoe Chace>Initially, when Velly Ray came around, Chandler would do the thing that he does. He talked to him, speechifying about political corruption and capitalism, unbidden. And Chandler says, at some point, Velly just started calling him the leader of Antifa.
<v Zoe Chace>起初，当维利·雷出现时，钱德勒会做他擅长的事。他会和他交谈，不由自主地发表关于政治腐败和资本主义的演讲。钱德勒说，到了某个时刻，维利就开始叫他反法西斯主义者的头目。

00:46:02.910 --> 00:46:27.910
<v Chandler Patey>He started it. And then other people started repeating it because it got them clicks, right? And then as that kept happening, and then they found out that I live right here in the so-called Antifa safe house, and then they came up with all sorts of weird fan fiction stories that got more clicks, if you just say "leader of Antifa" in them. So it didn't matter that it was me. They just wanted that word.
<v Chandler Patey>是他先开始的。然后其他人开始重复，因为它能带来点击量，对吧？随着这种情况持续发生，他们发现我住在这个所谓的“反法西斯主义者避难所”，然后他们编造了各种奇怪的同人小说，只要标题里带上“反法西斯主义者头目”，就能获得更多点击量。所以是不是我并不重要。他们只是想要那个词。

00:46:27.910 --> 00:46:32.690
<v Zoe Chace>Well, you're a main character. They needed-- you are a main character in the story of the protest.
<v Zoe Chace>好吧，你是个主角。他们需要——你是抗议故事里的一个主角。

00:46:32.690 --> 00:46:41.510
<v Chandler Patey>That is true. It is easier to give a face to something to get more clicks, right? Yeah.
<v Chandler Patey>那是真的。给事物一张脸以获得更多点击量更容易，对吧？是的。

00:46:41.510 --> 00:46:48.430
<v Zoe Chace>This is going to sound facetious, but it's not. Was there anything actually flattering about that?
<v Zoe Chace>这听起来可能有点轻浮，但并非如此。对此有什么感到受宠若惊的地方吗？

00:46:48.430 --> 00:47:12.240
<v Chandler Patey>A little bit. It was like a joke that my friends and I-- we all thought it was hilarious at first, and then we realized that, oh, that's actually not funny anymore. And so now I actually get mad at my friends or other protesters in general if they even joke about it anymore, because it's like, stop saying that. That's really, really annoying. It makes me look super conceited if that joke continues.
<v Chandler Patey>有一点点。起初这就像我和朋友们之间的一个笑话——我们都觉得这太搞笑了，然后我们意识到，噢，那其实一点也不好笑。所以现在，如果我的朋友或其他抗议者再开这种玩笑，我真的会生气，因为那是，别再说了。这太讨厌了。如果那个玩笑继续下去，这会让我看起来非常自负。

00:47:12.240 --> 00:47:23.120
<v Zoe Chace>The other reason to get mad at this joke, of course, is that it seems it'd be dangerous to be called the leader of Antifa by people who have a direct line to the White House and the director of the FBI.
<v Zoe Chace>当然，对这个玩笑感到生气的另一个原因是，被那些可以直接通向白宫和联邦调查局局长的人称为“反法西斯主义者头目”，这似乎很危险。

00:47:23.120 --> 00:47:26.540
<v Zoe Chace>I'm surprised the FBI hasn't been here already. Or have they?
<v Zoe Chace>我很惊讶FBI还没来过这里。或者他们来过？

00:47:26.540 --> 00:47:51.650
<v Chandler Patey>I mean, they're at ICE sometimes. I mean, there are people who claim to be FBI at ICE sometimes. And I've been detained while the FBI was there. And then they talked to me and asked me, so how are you organized, and who's your leader? Is someone paying you? Which is all hilarious shit for the FBI to be asking. Are you that-- are you incompetent? Are you stupid? Are you pretending to be stupid or do you think that I'm stupid? Which is it?
<v Chandler Patey>我是说，他们有时会出现在ICE那边。我是说，确实有人声称是FBI的人有时在ICE。我有一次被扣留时FBI就在场。然后他们跟我谈话，问我：你们是怎么组织的，谁是你的头目？有人付钱给你吗？这对FBI来说全是搞笑的鬼话。你是那样的——你们无能吗？你们蠢吗？你们是在装蠢，还是觉得我蠢？到底是哪一个？

00:47:51.650 --> 00:47:55.010
<v Zoe Chace>Think they're trying to get you to admit to being the leader of Antifa?
<v Zoe Chace>觉得他们是想诱导你承认是反法西斯主义者的头目？

00:47:55.010 --> 00:48:23.490
<v Chandler Patey>Not me. They asked-- for a while, they would ask everybody those-- basically those same questions. It was just really funny because it's like, obviously, we're not being paid. What are you talking about? Can't you just like, look at all of our private information? And then two alleged FBI agents gave me a brief interview where I didn't really answer any of the questions and just asked them how they feel about capitalism and corporate oligarchy and things, and just disregarded everything they said. It was pretty funny.
<v Chandler Patey>不是对我。他们问——有一段时间，他们会问每个人这些问题。这真太搞笑了，因为显然，我们没被付钱。你在说什么？难道你们不能直接查看我们所有的私人信息吗？然后两个据称是联邦调查局特工的人给了我一个简短的采访，我没真正回答任何问题，只是问他们对资本主义和企业寡头之类的感觉如何，然后就无视了他们说的一切。这挺有趣的。

00:48:23.490 --> 00:48:34.050
<v Zoe Chace>You're playing into what they think is Antifa, you know, when you go in there to an ICE facility and you're talking to DHS and you're talking about how we need to get rid of rich people?
<v Zoe Chace>当你在ICE设施里对国土安全部谈论我们需要摆脱富人时，你这是在配合他们对反法西斯主义者的设想，你知道吗？

00:48:34.050 --> 00:48:52.820
<v Chandler Patey>I'd say get rid of is not accurate, though. Because I'm a Democratic socialist. I like democracy. What we need isn't to just go to the rich people with a small group of violent Antifa rebels and then kill them. That's dumb. That won't result in lasting change. What we need is to convince everyone to--
<v Chandler Patey>我会说“摆脱”这个词不准确。因为我是民主社会主义者。我喜欢民主。我们需要做的不是带着一小群暴力的反法西斯主义叛军去找富人然后杀掉他们。那很蠢。那不会带来持久的变革。我们需要的是说服每个人去——

00:48:52.820 --> 00:49:04.980
<v Zoe Chace>This next piece of economic analysis from Chandler is going to play differently to you once I tell you what his father did for a living. So just listen a little closer than you maybe were going to, OK?
<v Zoe Chace>一旦我告诉你钱德勒的父亲是干什么的，这段关于经济的分析对你来说听起来会有所不同。所以听得比你可能打算听的再仔细一点，好吗？

00:49:04.980 --> 00:49:23.460
<v Chandler Patey>Rich people hate us. They will do everything they can to consolidate power. They will destroy the United States economy and therefore the global economy, so that they can drive everybody out of business and then buy up fucking all of the shit that people need to sell for the lowest possible price and just keep consolidating power over and over and over.
<v Chandler Patey>富人恨我们。他们会尽其所能巩固权力。他们会摧毁美国经济，进而摧毁全球经济，这样他们就可以把每个人都赶出局，然后用最低的价格买下人们需要出售的操蛋所有东西，并一遍又一遍地巩固权力。

00:49:23.460 --> 00:49:31.500
<v Zoe Chace>Now, Chandler was a kid during the financial crisis of 2008, and he saw a very particular side of it in his own house.
<v Zoe Chace>现在，钱德勒在2008年金融危机期间还是个孩子，他在自己家里看到了它非常具体的一面。

00:49:31.500 --> 00:49:41.260
<v Chandler Patey>So my family was-- started off being kind of wealthy up until-- so my dad worked at AIG Merrill Lynch.
<v Chandler Patey>所以我的家庭——起初挺有钱的，直到——我爸爸在AIG美林工作。

00:49:41.260 --> 00:49:42.220
<v Man 6>Oh!
<v Man 6>噢！

00:49:42.220 --> 00:49:58.670
<v Zoe Chace>That "oh" is the sound of someone thinking, oh, your dad was in the business of mortgage-backed securities that crashed the economy 17 years ago, when you were at a young and impressionable adolescent age. And now you're a committed socialist. Oh.
<v Zoe Chace>那个“噢”声是一个人的反应，意思是，噢，你爸爸在从事那个17年前摧毁经济的抵押贷款支持证券业务，当时你还是个年轻、易受影响的青春期少年。现在你是个坚定的社会主义者。噢。

00:49:58.670 --> 00:50:24.190
<v Chandler Patey>So and then was a higher up, like a marketing director or some shit like that. And so we had a pretty big house that they built and designed themselves, because my grandpa was a structural engineer. My grandma is like a designer-- or an interior designer or something. My mom also has a degree in interior design or something. And so they-- pretty wealthy. Not super rich, but pretty wealthy.
<v Chandler Patey>所以后来是高层，营销总监什么的。所以我们有一栋很大的房子，是他们自己建造和设计的，因为我爷爷是结构工程师。我奶奶是个室内设计师什么的。我妈妈也有室内设计学位什么的。所以他们——挺有钱的。不是超级有钱，但挺有钱的。

00:50:24.190 --> 00:50:44.150
<v Chandler Patey>But I don't know if you know, but AIG Merrill Lynch was one of the parties mainly responsible for the 2008 financial crisis. So when that happened, my dad lost everything and then went crazy and became a crippling alcoholic. And then I just didn't really see him very much ever again, which was fine, because I never really saw him very much to begin with. And he was kind of weird.
<v Chandler Patey>但我不知道你是否知道，AIG美林是导致2008年金融危机的主要当事人之一。所以当那件事发生时，我爸爸失去了一切，然后疯了，变成了严重的酗酒者。然后我就没再见过他多少次了，这倒无所谓，因为我本来就没怎么见过他。而且他有点怪。

00:50:44.150 --> 00:50:45.480
<v Zoe Chace>I'm sorry.
<v Zoe Chace>我很抱歉。

00:50:45.480 --> 00:50:46.960
<v Chandler Patey>Oh, it's fine.
<v Chandler Patey>噢，没关系。

00:50:46.960 --> 00:51:15.080
<v Zoe Chace>Just to say, Chandler denies his economic analysis comes from his personal experience. He says it comes from observations about history. Fair enough, nonetheless. Chandler does not like to talk about his family much. He says he doesn't really keep in touch with them. He grew up Mormon, just outside Portland. His dad died earlier this year. He has found a family of sorts here at the ICE facility. Sorry if that's corny to say.
<v Zoe Chace>只是说，钱德勒否认他的经济分析源于个人经历。他说这是源于对历史的观察。说得有理，尽管如此。钱德勒不太喜欢谈论他的家庭。他说他并没有真正和他们保持联系。他在波特兰郊外长大，是个摩门教徒。他爸爸今年早些时候去世了。他在ICE设施这里找到了某种家庭。如果这听起来很陈词滥调的话，我很抱歉。

00:51:15.080 --> 00:51:39.800
<v Zoe Chace>While we were there for a few hours, people stopped by and changed their clothes, used the bathroom. Someone gave him $10 for the water bill. A big wagon of medical supplies was sitting in the middle of the furniture-less living room. People had dragged it here after the camp was taken down by the cops. Some helped themselves to beef soup Chandler had on the stove. Not the vegan, of course, who called it flesh soup.
<v Zoe Chace>当我们呆了几个小时期间，人们进来换衣服，使用洗手间。有人给了他10美元交水费。一辆装满医疗用品的大车放在没有任何家具的客厅中间。在帐篷被警察拆除后，人们把它拖到了这里。一些人吃着钱德勒在炉子上炖的牛肉汤。当然，不是那个称之为“肉汤”的纯素食主义者吃的。

00:51:39.800 --> 00:52:09.930
<v Zoe Chace>Chandler found a partner, a romantic partner down here at the protest. His first significant romantic partner ever, he says. They came by for a second. In any case, Chandler has a sign up on his bedroom door that says, "Don't go in my room. That's fucking weird." Implying that enough people came by and tried to go in his room that he had to put a sign up. Also, Chandler is autistic. He calls himself very, very autistic.
<v Zoe Chace>钱德勒在抗议活动中找到了一个伴侣，一个浪漫的伴侣。他说这是他有史以来第一个重要的浪漫伴侣。他们过来看了一会儿。总之，钱德勒在他卧室门上贴了个牌子，上面写着：“别进我的房间。那太他妈奇怪了。”这意味着之前有足够多的人进来并试图进他的房间，他才不得不贴个牌子。另外，钱德勒是自闭症患者。他称自己是非常、非常自闭。

00:52:09.930 --> 00:52:30.490
<v Zoe Chace>And something that he attributes to his autism is his ability to talk and talk and talk very calmly to whomever is talking to him, however they're talking to him, even if, in my opinion, it very much makes him look like the leader of Antifa. Like our night with the streamers, when he gave that press conference of sorts, standing in his doorway.
<v Zoe Chace>他把这种能力归功于他的自闭症，那就是无论对方怎么跟他说，他都能非常冷静地与对方滔滔不绝地交谈，即使在我看来，这让他看起来非常像反法西斯主义者的头目。就像我们和直播者在一起的那个晚上，当他站在门口举行某种新闻发布会时。

00:52:30.490 --> 00:52:59.660
<v Zoe Chace>I mean, you coming out was this incredible catharsis. And they were talking to us like this was spiking the football. It was a victory lap. The camp was down and now they were teasing, bullying, yelling at the head of Antifa at the Antifa safe house, wildly outnumbering him, and making fun of him and his friends. And why would you give them that content?
<v Zoe Chace>我的意思是，你出来真是个令人难以置信的宣泄。他们谈论我们就像在把球扣进地里。这是一场胜利之旅。营地拆了，现在他们正在戏弄、欺负、对避难所里的反法西斯主义者头目叫喊，人数上远远超过他，还取笑他和他的朋友。你为什么要给他们那种素材？

00:52:59.660 --> 00:53:03.740
<v Chandler Patey>The content that I'm giving them is good for me.
<v Chandler Patey>我给他们的内容对我来说是好的。

00:53:03.740 --> 00:53:04.420
<v Zoe Chace>Why?
<v Zoe Chace>为什么？

00:53:04.420 --> 00:53:17.580
<v Chandler Patey>Because no matter how many people I've failed to convince, it is almost guaranteed that there is at least some people in their audience who responded positively to what I said.
<v Chandler Patey>因为无论我没能说服多少人，几乎可以肯定的是，他们的观众中至少有一些人会对我说的话做出积极回应。

00:53:17.580 --> 00:53:19.220
<v Zoe Chace>How do you figure?
<v Zoe Chace>你怎么看出来的？

00:53:19.220 --> 00:53:32.180
<v Chandler Patey>Because I have seen it. I go to their comment sections. I look at their comment sections. Fucking especially on Velly Ray's videos. There were so many of his fucking viewers that actually were on my side.
<v Chandler Patey>因为我亲眼看到了。我去了他们的评论区。我看了他们的评论区。特别是在维利·雷的视频下面。有那么多他的观众其实站在我这一边。

00:53:32.180 --> 00:53:39.520
<v Zoe Chace>So you really think that the commenters on the live streams are turning into Democratic Socialists some of them?
<v Zoe Chace>所以你真的认为直播间的评论者中，有些人正在转变成民主社会主义者？

00:53:39.520 --> 00:53:47.040
<v Chandler Patey>No, no, no. That would be crazy. I would love that, but no. I can only convince them on one point at a time. And--
<v Chandler Patey>不，不，不。那太疯狂了。我乐见其成，但没有。我一次只能说服他们一点。然后——

00:53:47.040 --> 00:54:14.120
<v Zoe Chace>Chandler isn't always so above it all. He admits he takes the bait sometimes and gets aggressive, physically aggressive, which of course gives the streamers exactly what they're looking for, which is footage of the violent Antifa. He pulled up a video of a fight he definitely participated in with that provocateur guy Rhein we talked about earlier, and someone else called Right Side Rebel, who has long, swishy, blonde hair.
<v Zoe Chace>钱德勒并不总是那么超然。他承认他有时会上钩并变得好斗，身体上的好斗，这当然给了直播者他们想要的东西，即暴力的反法西斯主义者的画面。他调出了一段视频，内容是他确实参与了我们之前提到的那个挑衅者莱恩的打斗，还有另一个叫“右侧叛逆”的人，长着长长的、晃动的金发。

00:54:14.120 --> 00:54:31.100
<v Chandler Patey>And then I get pulled off of Right Side Rebel. Everybody pulls me off of Right Side Rebel. And then I restrain Right Side Rebel again, and then it fizzles out after that. And--
<v Chandler Patey>然后我被从“右侧叛逆”身上拉开了。每个人都把我从“右侧叛逆”身上拉开。然后我再次控制住“右侧叛逆”，之后就没下文了。然后——

00:54:31.100 --> 00:54:38.770
<v Zoe Chace>But don't you think-- I mean, this is exactly what people are out there to stream. This is what they want to see.
<v Zoe Chace>但你不认为——我的意思是，这就是人们在外面直播的东西。这就是他们想看到的。

00:54:38.770 --> 00:54:48.290
<v Chandler Patey>There was six more-- I don't know how many more fights that night. But yeah, afterwards, I went and I apologized to Right Side Rebel and we smoothed it over.
<v Chandler Patey>那晚还有六次——我不知道还有多少次打斗。但是的，事后，我去了并向“右侧叛逆”道歉，我们把它摆平了。

00:54:48.290 --> 00:54:51.230
<v Chandler Patey>I was holding you back. I apologize if it seemed like I was--
<v Chandler Patey>我当时是在拉住你。如果看起来像我是在——我很抱歉。

00:54:51.230 --> 00:54:52.550
<v Chandler Patey>(SUBJECT) RIGHT SIDE
<v Chandler Patey>右侧阵营

00:54:52.550 --> 00:54:53.670
<v Chandler Patey>Actually, I apologize.
<v Chandler Patey>其实，我道歉。

00:54:53.670 --> 00:54:56.930
<v Chandler Patey>(SUBJECT) RIGHT SIDE
<v Chandler Patey>右侧阵营

00:54:56.930 --> 00:54:59.470
<v Woman 5>You guys just united the country. Oh, my god.
<v Woman 5>你们俩刚刚团结了国家。天哪。

00:54:59.470 --> 00:55:00.230
<v Chandler Patey>I was just trying--
<v Chandler Patey>我只是在尝试——

00:55:00.230 --> 00:55:13.930
<v Chandler Patey>So there was no reason for us to stay mad at each other. And then we were very friendly with each other and had another interview with Right Side Rebel afterwards, just and we're hugging and stuff, showing that we are friends now.
<v Chandler Patey>所以我们没理由继续生对方的气。然后我们互相非常友好，事后还和“右侧叛逆”进行了另一次采访，只是拥抱什么的，显示我们现在是朋友了。

00:55:13.930 --> 00:55:15.610
<v Zoe Chace>Are you friends with Right Side Rebel?
<v Zoe Chace>你是“右侧叛逆”的朋友吗？

00:55:15.610 --> 00:55:20.570
<v Chandler Patey>Friends is a strong word, but I was friendly, right?
<v Chandler Patey>“朋友”是个很重的词，但我当时很友好，对吧？

00:55:20.570 --> 00:55:27.250
<v Zoe Chace>Just again, so this clip so easily could be on Fox, and maybe has been on Fox.
<v Zoe Chace>再次声明，这个视频片段太容易出现在福克斯新闻上了，也许确实已经在福克斯新闻上播出过。

00:55:27.250 --> 00:55:29.210
<v Chandler Patey>Probably been on, yeah.
<v Chandler Patey>可能已经播过了，是的。

00:55:29.210 --> 00:55:36.350
<v Zoe Chace>And is just exactly, exactly what the administration wants to see in order to carry out their policies.
<v Zoe Chace>这正好就是行政当局想要看到的，以便执行他们的政策。

00:55:36.350 --> 00:55:40.780
<v Chandler Patey>I absolutely regret this a lot. I was just so angry at Rhein.
<v Chandler Patey>我非常后悔这件事。我当时只是对莱恩太生气了。

00:55:40.780 --> 00:55:44.460
<v Zoe Chace>It's so small and intimate, really, what's happening here.
<v Zoe Chace>这里发生的事情真的是太小、太私密了。

00:55:44.460 --> 00:55:49.260
<v Chandler Patey>Yeah. Yeah, we know all of these people. Well, most of them.
<v Chandler Patey>是的。是的，我们认识这里所有的人。好吧，大部分人。

00:55:49.260 --> 00:55:56.440
<v Zoe Chace>And it's so small, it is really something that it travels up all the way to the White House.
<v Zoe Chace>而且它这么小，它竟然能一直传到白宫，这真的很了不起。

00:55:56.440 --> 00:55:57.060
<v Chandler Patey>Right.
<v Chandler Patey>没错。

00:55:57.060 --> 00:56:21.380
<v Zoe Chace>The point is, these people know each other. They're in the same cast. They perform the same play, night after night on the same small set. They're like the dancers in West Side Story, coming out, dancing together on a city block, then going home to their respective neighborhoods until the next night, when they're dancing, fighting in front of an audience again.
<v Zoe Chace>重点是，这些人互相认识。他们在同一个剧组里。他们每晚在同一个小布景上表演同一场戏。他们就像《西区故事》里的舞者，走出来，在街区上一起跳舞，然后回家回到各自的社区，直到第二天晚上，他们又会在观众面前边跳边打。

00:56:21.380 --> 00:56:59.510
<v Zoe Chace>These conflicts, these pint-sized scraps on a street corner, are not the sort of thing that, in the past, our government has sent the most powerful military in the history of the world to resolve. They're not the sort of fights that have led to anyone being labeled a domestic terror group, a top national security priority. Maybe there is evidence out there that Antifa deserves that sort of treatment. But it's not in Portland. Though I love the smell of napalm in the morning, there's no war here.
<v Zoe Chace>这些冲突，街角这些微不足道的摩擦，不是过去我们的政府会派遣史上最强大的军队去解决的那种事情。它们也不是那种会导致任何人被贴上国内恐怖组织标签、列入最高国家安全优先事项的斗争。也许外界有证据表明反法西斯主义者值得那种对待。但那不在波特兰。虽然我喜欢清晨汽油弹的味道，但这儿没有战争。

00:56:59.510 --> 00:57:44.880
<v Ira Glass>Zoe Chace is a producer on our show. Suzanne Gaber helped her report this story. In the weeks since they were in Portland in October, things have actually quieted down in front of the ICE facility. Fewer people out on the street. Some of the streamers have moved on to other cities, and other issues.
<v Ira Glass>佐伊·蔡斯是我们节目的制作人。苏珊娜·加伯协助她报道了这个故事。在她们十月份在波特兰后的几周里，ICE设施前的情况其实已经安静下来了。街上的人少了。一些直播者已经转战其他城市和其他议题。

00:57:44.880 --> 00:57:51.040
<v Ira Glass>Well, the program was produced today by Nadia Reiman and Suzanne Garber, was edited by Laura Starecheski, Nancy Updike, and me.
<v Ira Glass>好了，今天的节目由娜迪亚·雷曼和苏珊娜·加伯制作，劳拉·斯塔雷切斯基、南希·厄普代克和我剪辑。

00:57:51.040 --> 00:58:08.400
<v Ira Glass>The people who put our show together include Phia Bennin, Michael Comite, Ruthie Pettito, Katherine Rae Mondo, Stowe Nelson, Ryan Rumery, Ike Sriskandarajah, Frances Swanson, Christopher Swetala, Marisa Robertson-Textor, and Julie Whitaker. Our managing editor is Sarah Abdurrahman. Our senior editor's David Kestenbaum, our executive editor is Emanuele Berry.
